var e={"company:mcmeh":{intro:`Modelled on MCI, Microwave Communications Inc, a small American company with a bold idea: to carry business calls and data on its own chain of microwave radio towers instead of on the Bell System's wires. AT&T held a near monopoly on long-distance telephone service in the United States, and MCI set out to win the right to compete with it.`,how:`Microwave links beam signals from dish to dish between towers placed in line of sight, a few dozen kilometres apart, so a company can build a long route without digging a trench for cable. MCI planned private lines along the highway from Chicago to St Louis that truckers and firms could rent for voice and data, and it spent years arguing before regulators for permission to connect them.`,history:`Jack Goeken, a radio dealer from Joliet, Illinois, founded MCI in 1963 to build microwave towers between Chicago and St Louis. Bill McGowan, a New York businessman who joined in 1968, raised the money to turn it into a national network. In 1969 the Federal Communications Commission finally approved the first route, over AT&T's objections. In 1974 MCI sued AT&T for blocking its connections, and in 1980 a jury awarded it $1.8 billion. Its Friends & Family plan of 1991 gave discounts on calls to people a customer named, as long as they also joined MCI. In 1998 WorldCom bought MCI for about $37 billion, the largest takeover in history up to that time.`,facts:[`The 1980 jury award against AT&T was $1.8 billion, then a record for an antitrust case.`,`By 1990 MCI was the second-largest long-distance carrier in the United States, behind AT&T.`,`WorldCom paid about $37 billion for MCI in 1998.`]},"company:sprunt":{intro:`Modelled on US Sprint, the American long-distance telephone company that built the first nationwide network carried entirely on fibre-optic cable. It was formed by joining GTE's Sprint long-distance arm with the long-distance business of United Telecom, a group of rural phone companies from Kansas, and it went straight after AT&T and MCI on the quality of its lines.`,how:`Sprint carried long-distance calls for homes and businesses over its own glass fibre and digital switches, which made far less hiss and crackle than old copper and microwave routes. Its famous adverts dropped a pin beside a telephone to show how quiet the line was. It earned money from per-minute call charges and from the private data networks it ran for companies.`,history:`Sprint's oldest root was the Brown Telephone Company, founded in Abilene, Kansas, in 1899, which grew into United Telecom. Its name came from the Southern Pacific railway, whose telecom arm sold long-distance calls over lines beside its tracks, and which GTE bought in 1983. In 1986 GTE Sprint and United Telecom's long-distance arm merged as US Sprint, and its new network was the first in the United States to run entirely on fibre. In 1992 United Telecom, by then its full owner, renamed itself Sprint Corporation. In 2005 Sprint merged with Nextel in a deal worth about $35 billion. In 2020 Sprint was folded into T-Mobile US.`,facts:[`The pin-drop adverts of 1986 made Sprint a byword for clear calls.`,`The Sprint and Nextel merger of 2005 was worth about $35 billion.`,`T-Mobile completed its takeover of Sprint on 1 April 2020.`]},"company:worldcon":{intro:`Modelled on WorldCom, which began as LDDS, Long Distance Discount Services, a small company in Mississippi that resold long-distance calls. Founded by a few local businessmen who met in a coffee shop in Hattiesburg, it bought calling capacity in bulk from the big carriers and resold it to local firms at a discount, a trade opened up by the break-up of the Bell System.`,how:`LDDS leased lines and minutes from AT&T and others in bulk and sold them on more cheaply to small businesses across the American South. It grew mainly by buying other small long-distance resellers, paying with its own shares, and folding their customers into one company to cut costs.`,history:`LDDS was founded in Hattiesburg, Mississippi, in 1983, and Bernard Ebbers, a former basketball coach who owned motels, became its chief executive in 1985. In 1995 it bought the WilTel network and took the name WorldCom. In 1996 it bought MFS Communications, the owner of the UUNET internet backbone, for about $12 billion. In 1998 it bought MCI for about $37 billion and took over CompuServe's network business in a deal that passed CompuServe's online service to AOL. In June 2002 its own internal auditors exposed billions of dollars of hidden expenses, a fraud that grew to about $11 billion, and on 21 July 2002 WorldCom filed the largest bankruptcy in US history. In 2005 Ebbers was sentenced to 25 years in prison, and in 2006 Verizon bought what was left, renamed MCI.`,facts:[`WorldCom listed about $107 billion of assets in its bankruptcy filing of 2002.`,`In 2000 regulators blocked WorldCom's planned takeover of Sprint.`,`The accounting fraud uncovered in 2002 reached about $11 billion.`]},"company:doublecross":{intro:`Modelled on Global Crossing, an American company founded to lay new fibre-optic cables under the oceans and sell their capacity to telephone companies and internet providers. Its founder came from the world of leveraged buyouts, and he bet that the growing internet would need far more international bandwidth than the old clubs of national phone companies were willing to build.`,how:`Instead of waiting for a group of national carriers to agree on a cable, Global Crossing paid for its own, starting with a loop across the Atlantic, and sold fibre pairs and wavelengths to carriers in advance. It then used that money, loans and its soaring shares to build or buy links across the Americas, Europe and Asia.`,history:`Gary Winnick, a former bond salesman at Drexel Burnham Lambert, founded Global Crossing in 1997 with partners. Its first cable, Atlantic Crossing-1, linking the United States with Britain, the Netherlands and Germany, entered service in 1998 after only about 14 months of work. In 1999 it bought Frontier, a big phone company from Rochester, New York, and Global Marine, a fleet of cable-laying ships, but lost a bidding war for US West to Qwest. By 2001 so much fibre had been laid by so many companies that bandwidth prices collapsed. On 28 January 2002 Global Crossing filed for bankruptcy, one of the largest in US history at that time. In 2003 Singapore Technologies Telemedia took control, and in 2011 Level 3 Communications bought the company.`,facts:[`Atlantic Crossing-1 was built in about 14 months and opened in 1998.`,`Global Crossing spent about $15 billion on its network between 1997 and 2002.`,`Gary Winnick had sold shares worth more than $700 million before the 2002 collapse.`]},"company:britishtelecomedy":{intro:`Modelled on BT, which began as the telephone service of Britain's General Post Office, the government department that already ran the mail and the telegraph. When the Post Office took over the private National Telephone Company, it became the operator of almost every telephone line in the country, a state monopoly run by civil servants and answerable to Parliament.`,how:`The Post Office built and owned the exchanges, cables and even the telephone sets, which customers rented rather than bought. It charged a rental for each line and a fee for each call, and long-distance trunk calls were placed by operators. Its own engineers designed and tested much of the equipment, and it shared the cost of cables to other countries with foreign phone companies.`,history:`In 1912 the General Post Office took over the National Telephone Company and became Britain's telephone operator everywhere except the city of Hull. In 1956 it was a partner with AT&T and Canada in TAT-1, the first transatlantic telephone cable, and it joined every TAT cable after that. In 1962 its Goonhilly earth station in Cornwall helped receive the first live television pictures sent across the Atlantic by the Telstar satellite. In 1981 the telephones were split from the Post Office as British Telecommunications. In 1984 the government sold just over half of it to the public, then the largest share sale in history. In 2016 BT bought EE, Britain's biggest mobile operator, for £12.5 billion.`,facts:[`TAT-1 could carry 36 telephone calls at once when it opened in 1956.`,`The 1984 share sale raised about £3.9 billion from over two million buyers.`,`The Post Office Tower in London opened in 1965 at 177 metres, then Britain's tallest building.`]},"company:nippontt":{intro:`Modelled on NTT, Nippon Telegraph and Telephone, the public corporation set up to rebuild and run Japan's telegraph and telephone network after the destruction of the Second World War. It held a monopoly on calls inside the country, with the duty of bringing a telephone line to every household, and its research laboratories worked on everything from switches to cables.`,how:`NTT owned Japan's exchanges and lines and earned its money from line rental and call charges, while calls abroad were left to a separate company. It bought its equipment from a family of Japanese makers, NEC, Fujitsu, Hitachi and Oki, which grew strong on its orders, and its own laboratories set the designs they built.`,history:`Japan's telephone service was reorganised in 1952 as the Nippon Telegraph and Telephone Public Corporation. In 1979 NTT opened the world's first commercial cellular telephone network, for car phones in Tokyo. In 1985 it was turned into a company, and after its shares were sold to the public in 1987 it was for a time the most valuable company in the world. Its mobile arm, NTT DoCoMo, began trading in 1992 and launched i-mode in February 1999, which put e-mail and simple web pages on ordinary phones. In October 2001 DoCoMo opened FOMA, the world's first commercial 3G network. In 2020 NTT bought out DoCoMo's other shareholders for about $40 billion.`,facts:[`The 1979 Tokyo car phone service was the first cellular network in the world.`,`i-mode had about 10 million users by August 2000, 18 months after launch.`,`NTT spent about $40 billion buying back the rest of DoCoMo in 2020.`]},"company:vodafoam":{intro:`Modelled on Vodafone, the British mobile phone operator that grew out of Racal, an electronics firm based in Newbury, Berkshire, which won one of Britain's first two licences to run a cellular network. Its name blended voice, data and phone, and it competed head-on with Cellnet, the network owned by British Telecom and Securicor.`,how:`Vodafone built its own radio masts and exchanges, at first on the analogue TACS system, and earned money from monthly line rental and per-minute call charges, paying dealers to sign up customers. Its first users were business people with car phones and handsets the size of a brick, but it chased the mass market as phones grew smaller and cheaper.`,history:`Racal's Vodafone network carried its first call on 1 January 1985, when the comedian Ernie Wise rang the company's office in Newbury from St Katharine Docks in London. Racal sold part of Vodafone on the stock market in 1988 and split it off completely in 1991. In December 1992 an engineer working for Vodafone, Neil Papworth, sent the world's first text message, "Merry Christmas", from a computer to a colleague's phone. In 1999 it bought AirTouch of the United States for about $60 billion. In 2000 it took over Germany's Mannesmann for about $180 billion, the largest takeover ever made. In 2014 it sold its 45 per cent share of Verizon Wireless to Verizon for $130 billion.`,facts:[`The Mannesmann deal of 2000 was worth about $180 billion, a record takeover.`,`The first text message, sent in 1992, read "Merry Christmas".`,`Vodafone sold its stake in Verizon Wireless for $130 billion in 2014.`]},"company:chinamobbile":{intro:`Modelled on China Mobile, the state-controlled company that runs mobile phone networks across mainland China. It was created as a company listed in Hong Kong to take over, province by province, the mobile networks built by China's telecom ministry, and to raise money from foreign investors for one of the fastest-growing phone markets in the world.`,how:`China Mobile built and ran GSM networks, selling prepaid and contract service to people in the cities and the countryside, and it earned most of its money from calls and text messages. It bought network equipment from Western makers and from Chinese ones such as Huawei and ZTE, and the Chinese state kept control through a parent company.`,history:`In 1997 the mobile networks of Guangdong and Zhejiang were placed in a new company, China Telecom (Hong Kong), which listed its shares in Hong Kong and New York. In 2000 the government split mobile service off from China Telecom as China Mobile Communications Corporation, and the listed company took the China Mobile name. By 2006 it had more than 300 million subscribers, the most of any operator in the world. In 2009 it launched 3G service on TD-SCDMA, a standard developed in China. In 2019 it began commercial 5G service. By 2023 it served about a billion mobile customers.`,facts:[`China Mobile had more than 300 million subscribers by 2006.`,`By 2023 it served about 990 million mobile customers, more than any other operator.`]},"company:nokiaaa":{intro:`Modelled on Nokia, a Finnish company that began as a mill grinding wood into pulp for paper, founded by a mining engineer, Fredrik Idestam. It took its name from the Nokianvirta river near Tampere, whose rapids drove its machines, in a country covered with forests and then part of the Russian Empire.`,how:`The mill ground spruce logs into wood pulp and pressed it into board and paper, using the power of the rapids to turn its grinders. It sold much of its output to Russia and other markets of the empire. Its fortune rested on cheap timber, free water power and the growing demand for paper for newspapers, books and packaging.`,history:`Fredrik Idestam set up his first pulp mill in 1865 and opened a second at the Nokia rapids in 1868, and the company took the name Nokia in 1871. In 1967 it merged with Finnish Rubber Works, famous for its boots, and Finnish Cable Works to form the Nokia Corporation. In 1987 it launched the Mobira Cityman, a handheld phone weighing about 800 grams. In 1991 Finland's prime minister made the world's first GSM call on a network Nokia had helped build. In 1998 Nokia passed Motorola as the biggest maker of mobile phones in the world, and it held the lead until 2012, when Samsung overtook it. In 2014 it sold its phone business to Microsoft and kept its network equipment arm.`,facts:[`Snake, the famous phone game, first came on the Nokia 6110 in 1997.`,`By 2008 Nokia made about four in ten of the world's mobile phones.`,`The Nokia 1100 of 2003 sold more than 200 million units.`]},"company:errorsson":{intro:`Modelled on Ericsson, the Swedish company founded by Lars Magnus Ericsson, an instrument maker who opened a small workshop in Stockholm repairing telegraph equipment. The telephone had just been invented in America, and Ericsson was soon building telephones of his own, among the first in Europe to do so.`,how:`Ericsson made telephone sets, switchboards and the parts needed to link them, and sold them to telephone companies in Sweden and abroad. Because the Swedish market was small, it relied from the start on exports, and it often won contracts by helping customers find the money to build their networks.`,history:`Lars Magnus Ericsson opened his repair shop in Stockholm in 1876 and began making his own telephones in 1878. In 1932 the collapse of the financier Ivar Kreuger, who had secretly gained control of Ericsson, nearly ruined it, and the Wallenberg family's bank stepped in. In 1976 it launched AXE, a modular digital exchange that it sold to phone companies around the world. In 1981 it supplied the switches for NMT, the first mobile network shared by several countries, in the Nordic region. In 2001 it put its handsets into a joint venture with Sony, Sony Ericsson. In 2012 Sony bought Ericsson's half, leaving it to concentrate on network equipment.`,facts:[`Ericsson bought Nortel's North American wireless business for $1.13 billion in 2009.`,`Around 2010 Ericsson said about 40 per cent of the world's mobile traffic passed through its networks.`,`Sony paid €1.05 billion for Ericsson's half of Sony Ericsson in 2012.`]},"company:motorollover":{intro:`Modelled on Motorola, the Chicago company founded by the brothers Paul and Joseph Galvin as the Galvin Manufacturing Corporation. Its first product was a battery eliminator, a box that let battery-powered home radios run from mains electricity, and it was soon looking for new places to put a radio.`,how:`Galvin sold radio power supplies through radio shops, and its engineers worked out how to fit a radio into a car, coping with engine noise and bumpy roads. The car radio was sold as the Motorola, a name blending motor with Victrola, a popular record player. Rugged radios for vehicles and police cars became its speciality.`,history:`Paul and Joseph Galvin founded Galvin Manufacturing in Chicago in 1928, and in 1930 it launched the Motorola car radio. In 1943 it built the SCR-300 backpack radio for the US Army, and in 1947 the company renamed itself Motorola. In 1969 a Motorola radio relayed Neil Armstrong's first words from the Moon. In 1973 Martin Cooper of Motorola made the first public call on a handheld mobile phone in New York, and in 1984 the DynaTAC 8000X went on sale for $3,995. Its StarTAC flip phone of 1996 and slim RAZR of 2004 were huge hits, and it led the building of the Iridium satellite phone system in the 1990s. In 2011 it split in two, and in 2012 Google bought the phone half, Motorola Mobility, for $12.5 billion.`,facts:[`The DynaTAC 8000X of 1984 weighed about 800 grams and cost $3,995.`,`Motorola's 68000 chip of 1979 powered the first Apple Macintosh in 1984.`,`The RAZR V3 of 2004 sold more than 130 million units.`]},"company:nortail":{intro:`Modelled on Nortel, which began as Northern Electric, the factory arm of the Bell Telephone Company of Canada. It was set up in Montreal so that Canada's main phone company could make its own telephones, switchboards, wire and cable instead of importing them from the United States.`,how:`Northern Electric made and installed the telephone sets, switchboards and cables for the Bell network in Canada, and most of its money came from its owner's orders. For many years it built designs licensed from Western Electric, the manufacturer for America's Bell System, before it began to design its own equipment.`,history:`Northern Electric and Manufacturing was founded in Montreal in 1895 to make equipment for Bell Canada. In 1956 an American antitrust settlement made Western Electric give up its stake, and Northern Electric began to design its own exchanges. In 1976 it renamed itself Northern Telecom and promised a full range of digital switches, and its DMS-100 for large exchanges followed in 1979. During the dot-com boom it sold a huge share of the world's optical networking gear, and by 2000 its shares made up about a third of the value of the Toronto Stock Exchange. Its shares fell by more than 90 per cent by 2002, and accounting scandals followed in 2004. On 14 January 2009 Nortel filed for bankruptcy protection, and in 2011 its patents were sold for $4.5 billion.`,facts:[`In 2000 Nortel alone made up about a third of the Toronto Stock Exchange's value.`,`A group led by Apple and Microsoft paid $4.5 billion for Nortel's patents in 2011.`]},"company:corny":{intro:`Modelled on Corning, the American glassmaker founded by Amory Houghton, a Massachusetts businessman, which moved to the town of Corning in upstate New York and took its name. It specialised in technical glass, such as lamp chimneys, coloured lenses for railway signals and tubes for thermometers, rather than ordinary bottles and windows.`,how:`Corning melted sand with carefully measured mixes of other ingredients to give glass special properties: resistance to heat, exact colours or great clarity. It sold mostly to other manufacturers rather than to shoppers, and its profits came from kinds of glass that rivals could not easily make. It hired scientists to invent new glasses rather than relying on craftsmen's recipes.`,history:`Amory Houghton started the business in 1851 in Massachusetts and moved it to Corning, New York, in 1868. From 1880 it made glass bulbs for Thomas Edison's electric lamps. In 1915 it launched Pyrex, glass that could go from a cold room into a hot oven without cracking. In 1970 three of its scientists, Robert Maurer, Donald Keck and Peter Schultz, made the first optical fibre pure enough to carry light signals over long distances. In 2007 its toughened Gorilla Glass, revived from a project of the 1960s, covered the screen of Apple's first iPhone. By 2020 Corning was one of the world's biggest makers of optical fibre.`,facts:[`The 1970 fibre lost less than 20 decibels of light per kilometre, good enough for telephone links.`,`Corning cast the 200-inch mirror blank for the Palomar telescope in 1934.`]},"company:subcomedy":{intro:`Modelled on SubCom, the American maker and layer of undersea telecommunications cables, which began inside AT&T's Bell System. It designed the cables, the repeaters that boost the signal every few dozen kilometres along the sea bed, and the methods and ships used to lay them across whole oceans.`,how:`An ocean cable must work for decades at the bottom of the sea, where no one can repair it easily, so every repeater is sealed and built to the strictest standards and powered by electricity sent down the cable itself. The business built cables and equipment for groups of phone companies, which shared the cost of each new route and its capacity.`,history:`In 1955 and 1956 AT&T laid TAT-1, the first transatlantic telephone cable, from Scotland to Newfoundland, using repeaters designed at Bell Labs. In 1988 TAT-8, the first transatlantic cable to use optical fibre, went into service, built by AT&T with British and French partners. In 1997 AT&T's undersea business, by then part of Lucent Technologies, was sold to the conglomerate Tyco, which named it TyCom. In 2018 TE Connectivity, which had inherited it from Tyco, sold it to the investment firm Cerberus, and it took the name SubCom. By 2020 SubCom was one of three companies, with Alcatel Submarine Networks and NEC, that built most of the world's ocean cables.`,facts:[`TAT-8 could carry about 40,000 calls at once in 1988, over 1,000 times TAT-1's capacity.`,`Cerberus bought SubCom from TE Connectivity for $325 million in 2018.`]},"company:disco":{intro:`Modelled on Cisco Systems, the company founded by Leonard Bosack and Sandy Lerner, a married couple who ran computer networks in different departments of Stanford University. They built routers, boxes that pass data between networks of different kinds, and sold them at first to universities and research labs that wanted to join their networks together.`,how:`A router reads the address on each packet of data and decides which network to send it on to, so that separate local networks can act as one larger one. Cisco sold the boxes with its own operating software, which could handle several network protocols at once, and it made high profits because customers' networks came to depend on its equipment.`,history:`Bosack and Lerner founded Cisco in 1984, building on Stanford's "blue box" router, whose software had been written by William Yeager. In 1987 the venture capital firm Sequoia invested, and in 1990 the professional managers it brought in pushed both founders out. Cisco went public in February 1990 and grew by buying other companies, starting with the switch maker Crescendo in 1993. Under John Chambers, chief executive from 1995, it rode the internet boom, and in March 2000 it briefly became the world's most valuable company, worth over $500 billion. In 2001, after the bubble burst, it wrote off about $2.2 billion of equipment it could not sell.`,facts:[`Cisco's stock market value passed $500 billion in March 2000.`,`In 2001 Cisco wrote off about $2.2 billion of unsold stock.`]},"company:threecomma":{intro:`Modelled on 3Com, the networking company started by Robert Metcalfe, one of the inventors of Ethernet at Xerox's Palo Alto Research Center. Its name stood for computers, communication and compatibility, and its plan was to turn Ethernet, a way of linking the computers in an office with a shared cable, into an open standard and sell the equipment for it.`,how:`3Com made Ethernet adapter cards that plugged into computers, with the transceivers and cables to connect them, and the hubs that joined them into an office network. It won by persuading big computer makers to publish Ethernet as an open standard, so that any maker's machines could share one network, and then sold the cheapest, most widely supported cards.`,history:`Robert Metcalfe founded 3Com in 1979, the year he persuaded DEC, Intel and Xerox to back Ethernet as a common standard. In 1982 its EtherLink card for the IBM PC helped bring Ethernet to personal computers. It went public in 1984. In 1997 it bought US Robotics, the modem maker, for about $6.6 billion, which brought along the Palm Pilot organiser. In 2000 it spun off Palm, whose shares were briefly worth more than 3Com's own. In 2010 HP bought 3Com for $2.7 billion.`,facts:[`3Com paid about $6.6 billion for US Robotics in 1997.`,`On its first day of trading in 2000, Palm was valued at over $50 billion.`,`HP bought 3Com for $2.7 billion in 2010.`]},"company:junipurr":{intro:`Modelled on Juniper Networks, a Silicon Valley start-up founded by Pradeep Sindhu, a computer scientist from Xerox PARC, to build routers for the core of the internet. Its plan was to beat Cisco at the heaviest work, moving the floods of data between big internet providers, with machines designed for that task alone.`,how:`Juniper's routers split the job in two: custom chips forwarded packets at the full speed of the line, while a separate processor ran the routing software that worked out where packets should go. Its software, JUNOS, was built on the FreeBSD version of Unix. It sold mainly to large carriers and internet backbone operators, which bought fewer boxes but paid a great deal for each.`,history:`Pradeep Sindhu founded Juniper in Mountain View, California, in 1996. In September 1998 it shipped its first product, the M40, a backbone router that could handle 40 gigabits per second. It went public in June 1999, and its shares soared on the first day of trading. By 2001 it had won a large share of the market for core internet routers from Cisco. In 2024 Hewlett Packard Enterprise agreed to buy Juniper for about $14 billion, and the deal closed in 2025.`,facts:[`The M40 router of 1998 could carry 40 gigabits of data per second.`,`Hewlett Packard Enterprise agreed to pay about $14 billion for Juniper in 2024.`]},"company:hazy":{intro:`Modelled on Hayes Microcomputer Products, a company from Atlanta, Georgia, founded by Dennis Hayes and Dale Heatherington to make modems for the new hobby and personal computers. A modem turns computer data into sounds that can travel down an ordinary telephone line, and turns them back into data at the other end.`,how:`Hayes made modems that plugged into personal computers and could dial and answer the telephone by themselves, controlled by short text commands that began with AT, for attention: ATDT, for example, dialled a number using tones. It sold its modems through computer shops at a premium price, on a reputation for reliability.`,history:`Dennis Hayes and Dale Heatherington started the company in 1978, building modem boards at first at Hayes's dining room table in Atlanta. In 1981 they launched the Hayes Smartmodem, a 300 bit per second box that worked with any computer and introduced the AT commands. By 1985 rival makers advertised their own modems as "Hayes compatible", because its command set had become the standard. Cheaper competitors cut into its sales, and in 1994 Hayes filed for bankruptcy protection. It came out of it in 1996, went bankrupt again in 1998, and closed in 1999.`,facts:[`The 1981 Smartmodem sent 300 bits per second, about 30 characters.`,`By 1985 "Hayes compatible" was the standard claim on rival modems.`]},"company:robotalks":{intro:`Modelled on US Robotics, a modem maker from Skokie, Illinois, near Chicago, founded by a group of students and friends. Despite its science-fiction name, borrowed from the robot company in Isaac Asimov's stories, it made no robots: it built modems, the boxes that let computers talk over telephone lines.`,how:`US Robotics sold fast modems to businesses and to the people who ran dial-up bulletin boards, and cheaper ones to home users through computer stores. Its engineers pushed speeds up with their own signalling schemes before official standards caught up, which made its modems popular with people who paid by the minute to be online.`,history:`Casey Cowell and four friends founded US Robotics in Chicago in 1976. In 1986 its Courier HST modem reached 9,600 bits per second with its own high-speed method, which made it a favourite of bulletin board operators. In 1995 it bought Palm Computing, and in 1996 Palm's Pilot pocket organiser appeared. In 1997 its x2 technology reached 56 kilobits per second over ordinary phone lines. In 1997 3Com bought US Robotics for about $6.6 billion. In 2000 3Com spun the modem business off again under the US Robotics name.`,facts:[`The x2 modems of 1997 were almost 200 times faster than the 300 bit per second modems of 1981.`,`3Com bought US Robotics for about $6.6 billion in 1997.`]},"company:intelsplat":{intro:`Modelled on INTELSAT, the International Telecommunications Satellite Organization, set up by a treaty between governments to own and run satellites that relay telephone calls and television between continents. Each member country paid in money and received a share of the capacity and the profits, with the United States holding the largest share through its company Comsat.`,how:`INTELSAT bought communications satellites from American makers and placed them in geostationary orbit, where a satellite circles once a day and so seems to hang still above the equator. National phone companies built earth stations with giant dish aerials and rented circuits through the satellite, paying for each telephone channel or television hour they used.`,history:`INTELSAT was founded in August 1964 by a group of countries led by the United States, with Comsat as its manager. In April 1965 it launched Early Bird, or Intelsat I, the first commercial communications satellite in geostationary orbit, linking North America and Europe. In 1969 its Intelsat III satellites completed a ring over the Atlantic, Pacific and Indian Oceans, in time to carry the Apollo 11 Moon landing to viewers around the world. In 2001 INTELSAT was turned into a private company, and in 2005 it was bought by private equity firms. In 2020 it filed for bankruptcy protection. In 2025 SES of Luxembourg bought it for about $3.1 billion.`,facts:[`Early Bird could carry 240 telephone circuits or one television channel in 1965.`,`Early Bird weighed only about 35 kilograms at its launch in 1965.`,`SES bought Intelsat for about $3.1 billion in 2025.`]},"company:hugeaircraft":{intro:`Modelled on Hughes Aircraft, the aviation company that the young millionaire Howard Hughes set up as a division of his family's Hughes Tool Company in Southern California. It began by building racing aeroplanes to Hughes's own ideas, with the aim of breaking world speed records, and Hughes flew them himself.`,how:`Hughes Aircraft was paid for out of the profits of Hughes Tool, whose drill bits were used all over the oil industry, so at first it built a handful of record-breaking planes rather than selling aircraft by the thousand. Its engineers chased speed with clean streamlining, flush rivets and retractable landing gear, and it hired gifted designers with generous budgets.`,history:`Howard Hughes set up Hughes Aircraft in 1932, and in 1935 he flew its H-1 racer to a world speed record of about 352 miles per hour. In 1947 he flew its giant wooden flying boat, nicknamed the Spruce Goose, just once, for about a mile. In the 1950s the company turned to electronics, building radar, fire control systems and Falcon missiles for the US Air Force. In 1960 Theodore Maiman built the first working laser at Hughes Research Laboratories in Malibu. In 1963 Syncom 2, designed by Harold Rosen's team at Hughes, became the first satellite in geosynchronous orbit, and Hughes built Early Bird in 1965. In 1985 General Motors bought Hughes, and in 1994 its Hughes Electronics arm launched DirecTV, a satellite television service.`,facts:[`The H-1 racer set a landplane speed record of about 352 miles per hour in 1935.`,`General Motors paid about $5 billion for Hughes in 1985.`,`Boeing bought the Hughes satellite factory for $3.75 billion in 2000.`]},"company:iridumb":{intro:`Modelled on Iridium, the satellite telephone company spun out of Motorola to let people call from anywhere on Earth with a handheld phone. Instead of a few satellites high above the equator, it planned a swarm of them in low orbit, passing each call from one satellite to the next across the sky.`,how:`The satellites were to circle about 780 kilometres up, close enough for a handset to reach, and link to each other by radio so that a call could cross the world in space before coming down to a ground station. The system was named after the element iridium, whose atom has 77 electrons, the number of satellites first planned. It aimed at business travellers, ships, armies and explorers.`,history:`Three Motorola engineers, Bary Bertiger, Ray Leopold and Ken Peterson, came up with the idea in 1987, and Iridium was set up as a separate company in 1991. Service began on 1 November 1998 with 66 working satellites, at a cost of about $5 billion. Phones cost about $3,000 and calls several dollars a minute, so few customers signed up, and in August 1999, nine months after launch, Iridium filed for bankruptcy. In 2000 a group of investors bought the satellites, which Motorola had planned to steer down to burn up, for about $25 million, and service reopened in 2001. In 2019 it finished replacing the whole fleet with new Iridium NEXT satellites. In June 2026 the rocket company Rocket Lab agreed to buy Iridium in a deal worth about $8 billion.`,facts:[`The original network of 1998 cost about $5 billion to build and launch.`,`Investors bought the bankrupt network for about $25 million in 2000.`,`SpaceX launched 75 Iridium NEXT satellites between 2017 and 2019.`]},"company:teledelusion":{intro:`Modelled on Teledesic, a company backed by the mobile phone pioneer Craig McCaw and by Bill Gates of Microsoft, which planned an internet in the sky. Its idea was a huge network of satellites in low orbit giving fast data links to homes, schools and businesses anywhere on Earth, including places no cable would reach.`,how:`Teledesic's design called for hundreds of satellites in low orbits, linked to each other so that data could hop across the network like packets on the internet. Users would have small dish terminals on their roofs, and Teledesic expected to sell its capacity through local phone companies and service providers rather than directly to the public.`,history:`Teledesic began in 1990 as Calling Communications, and in March 1994 it announced a plan for 840 satellites costing about $9 billion. In 1997 the Federal Communications Commission licensed its radio frequencies, and Boeing joined as a partner as the design was cut to fewer, larger satellites. In 1998 it launched a single experimental satellite. The bankruptcy of Iridium in 1999 frightened investors away from satellite networks, and Teledesic tried to merge its plans with those of the rival ICO in 2000. In October 2002 Teledesic suspended work on its satellites without ever launching a working network.`,facts:[`The 1994 plan called for 840 satellites at a cost of about $9 billion.`,`Teledesic suspended its satellite work in October 2002.`]},"company:bolt":{intro:`Modelled on BBN, Bolt Beranek and Newman, a consulting firm in Cambridge, Massachusetts, founded by two professors from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Richard Bolt and Leo Beranek, with their former student Robert Newman. It began as an acoustics practice, designing concert halls and studying noise, and hired many bright scientists from the nearby universities.`,how:`BBN earned its money from consulting and research contracts, many of them from the US government, rather than from selling products. Its early work was on sound: the acoustics of halls and theatres and the noise of jet aircraft near airports. Its staff worked more like a university department than a company, free to follow their interests.`,history:`Bolt, Beranek and Newman was founded in 1948, and its first big job was the acoustics of the United Nations General Assembly hall in 1949. In 1957 the psychologist J C R Licklider joined, and in 1960 BBN bought the first PDP-1 computer that DEC sold. In 1969 it built the Interface Message Processors, the small computers that routed traffic on the ARPANET, and delivered the first to UCLA that summer. In 1971 Ray Tomlinson, a BBN engineer, sent the first e-mail between two networked computers and chose the @ sign for addresses. In 1997 GTE bought BBN. In 2009 Raytheon bought its research arm, BBN Technologies.`,facts:[`BBN won the ARPANET router contract in 1969, worth about $1 million.`,`The first ARPANET message, from UCLA to the Stanford Research Institute, crashed after two letters in 1969.`]},"company:tymshear":{intro:`Modelled on Tymshare, a timesharing company from Cupertino, California, founded by Tom O'Rourke and Dave Schmidt, two engineers who had left General Electric. It sold slices of time on its computers to engineers and businesses who could not afford a machine of their own, and they reached it over telephone lines from teletype terminals in their offices.`,how:`Timesharing let dozens of people share one large computer at once, each typing at a terminal while the machine switched between them many times a second, so each felt it was his own. Tymshare charged for connect time, computer time and storage, and sold ready-made programs for engineering sums, statistics and accounts as well as raw computing.`,history:`Tom O'Rourke and Dave Schmidt founded Tymshare in 1964, after General Electric refused to sell them a computer for their planned service. In 1966 it began running its own SDS 940, a machine built for timesharing using software from the University of California, Berkeley. In 1971 its engineers, led by LaRoy Tymes, brought into service Tymnet, a network of small computers that carried customers' typing to Tymshare's machines from cities across America. Through the 1970s it added DEC PDP-10 computers and became one of the largest timesharing firms in the country. In 1984 the aerospace company McDonnell Douglas bought Tymshare.`,facts:[`Tymnet's central supervisor program went into service in November 1971.`,`McDonnell Douglas bought Tymshare and Tymnet in 1984.`]},"company:edsel":{intro:`Modelled on EDS, Electronic Data Systems, founded in Dallas by Ross Perot, a former IBM salesman, with $1,000 of his own money. Its idea was facilities management: instead of selling computers, it would take over a customer's whole data processing department, run its machines and programs, and charge a fee for the job.`,how:`EDS signed long contracts, often of several years, to run the computer work of insurers, banks and government agencies. It hired its own staff, many of them military veterans, trained them hard and sent them into customers' offices, and it made money by running the work more cheaply and reliably than the customer had managed alone.`,history:`Ross Perot founded EDS in Dallas in 1962, after IBM showed no interest in his idea of selling computer services rather than machines. Contracts to process claims for Medicare and Medicaid, the health programmes created in 1965, made it rich. When EDS went public in 1968 its shares soared, making Perot one of the wealthiest men in Texas. In 1984 General Motors bought EDS for $2.5 billion to run the car maker's huge computer systems. Perot clashed with GM's managers, and in 1986 GM paid him about $700 million for his shares and his seat on its board. In 1996 GM spun EDS off as an independent company again. In 2008 Hewlett-Packard bought EDS for about $13.9 billion.`,facts:[`Ross Perot started EDS with $1,000 in 1962.`,`General Motors paid $2.5 billion for EDS in 1984.`,`Hewlett-Packard paid about $13.9 billion for EDS in 2008.`]},"company:ge":{intro:`Modelled on General Electric, the American electrical giant formed by merging Thomas Edison's company with its rival Thomson-Houston. It made the dynamos, power stations, motors, street lights and light bulbs that were electrifying America's cities and factories, and it held many of the most important patents in the field.`,how:`GE built and sold electrical equipment of almost every kind, from huge generators for power companies to lamps for homes, and earned money from both the machines and the patents behind them. It invested heavily in research to stay ahead of rivals such as Westinghouse, and it trained engineers who spread its methods across American industry.`,history:`General Electric was formed in 1892 from the merger of Edison General Electric and Thomson-Houston. In 1959 it built ERMA, a computer system that read the magnetic ink numbers on cheques for the Bank of America. In 1964 GE computers at Dartmouth College ran the first BASIC programs, on a timesharing system that let students type at terminals across the campus. In 1965 it began selling timesharing to businesses, the start of GE Information Services, and it built the GE-645 for Multics, a timesharing project with MIT and Bell Labs. In 1970 GE sold its mainframe computer business to Honeywell but kept its timesharing network. In 1985 that network launched GEnie, an online service for home computer owners.`,facts:[`The first BASIC programs ran on a GE-225 at Dartmouth in May 1964.`,`GE sold its mainframe computer business to Honeywell in 1970.`]},"company:compuswerve":{intro:`Modelled on CompuServe, which began as Compu-Serv Network, a computer timesharing business set up inside Golden United Life Insurance in Columbus, Ohio. It was meant to handle the insurer's own data processing and to sell spare time on its computers to other businesses that dialled in from terminals in their offices.`,how:`CompuServe ran DEC PDP-10 computers and rented time on them by the hour to companies, which used them for accounts, engineering and other number work. Business customers kept the machines busy during office hours, but they sat mostly idle in the evenings and at weekends, which left the company looking for something to fill them.`,history:`Jeff Wilkins set up Compu-Serv in Columbus in 1969 for Golden United Life Insurance. In 1979 it began selling its idle evening time to home computer owners as MicroNET, one of the first consumer online services. In 1980 the tax preparation firm H&R Block bought the company. Its CB Simulator of 1980 was one of the first online chat services, and in 1987 a CompuServe programmer, Steve Wilhite, created the GIF picture format. By 1995 it had about 3 million members, but cheaper, friendlier rivals such as AOL were overtaking it. In 1998 WorldCom bought CompuServe, kept its network and passed its online service to AOL.`,facts:[`The GIF picture format was created at CompuServe in 1987.`,`CompuServe had about 3 million members in 1995.`]},"company:prodigal":{intro:`Modelled on Prodigy, an online service started as a joint venture of IBM, the broadcaster CBS and the retailer Sears. It was meant to bring news, shopping, banking and games to ordinary households through their home computers and telephone lines, paid for partly by advertising, like a newspaper on the screen.`,how:`Prodigy drew colourful pages with simple graphics on the subscriber's own computer, so the slow phone line only had to carry instructions, not whole pictures, and it filled a strip at the bottom of every screen with adverts. It charged a flat monthly fee rather than by the hour, and hoped to earn as much from advertisers and shopping as from members.`,history:`IBM, CBS and Sears formed the venture in 1984, and CBS dropped out in 1986. Prodigy opened in a few cities in 1988 and across the United States in 1990. In 1991 it began charging extra for members who sent more than 30 e-mails a month, which angered many users. In 1995 a New York court ruled that because Prodigy moderated its message boards, it could be held responsible for what its users posted, a decision that led Congress to pass the law known as Section 230 in 1996. IBM and Sears sold Prodigy in 1996 for a small fraction of the more than $1 billion they had spent on it. In 2001 the phone company SBC bought it.`,facts:[`IBM and Sears spent more than $1 billion on Prodigy before selling it in 1996.`,`In 1991 Prodigy began charging for e-mails beyond 30 a month.`]},"company:americaoffline":{intro:`Modelled on AOL, America Online, which began as Quantum Computer Services, a company near Washington, DC, running an online service for owners of Commodore 64 home computers. It offered chat, games, messages and news through friendly picture screens, aiming at ordinary families rather than computer experts.`,how:`Members dialled in with a modem and AOL's own software, which drew the menus on their computer so the phone line only had to carry text and commands. It charged a monthly fee plus an hourly rate, and it made being online feel sociable, with chat rooms, member profiles and a cheerful voice announcing new mail.`,history:`Quantum Computer Services launched Q-Link for Commodore 64 owners in November 1985. In 1989 its service for Apple and IBM-compatible computers was named America Online, and in 1991 the company took that name. From 1993 it mailed free trial disks, and then CDs, to millions of homes. In December 1996 it switched to unlimited use for $19.95 a month, and so many members logged on that they met busy signals for weeks. In January 2001 it completed its merger with the media giant Time Warner, a deal valued at about $165 billion when it was announced in 2000. In 2002 AOL Time Warner wrote off about $99 billion, then the biggest annual loss in American corporate history.`,facts:[`The unlimited plan of 1996 cost $19.95 a month.`,`The Time Warner merger was valued at about $165 billion in 2000.`,`AOL Time Warner reported a loss of about $99 billion for 2002.`]},"company:uunot":{intro:`Modelled on UUNET, a company from Falls Church, Virginia, founded by Rick Adams to carry Usenet news and e-mail between Unix computers that dialled each other over the phone. It began as a non-profit service for the Unix community, set up because the volunteer machines that passed this traffic along were overloaded.`,how:`Unix computers of the time swapped mail and news using UUCP, copying files to each other over dial-up lines, so a message could take days to hop across the country. UUNET ran a central hub that sites could call to send and fetch their traffic, charging fees to cover its costs, and it paid for long-distance links so that news no longer had to crawl from machine to machine.`,history:`Rick Adams founded UUNET in 1987 as a non-profit, with backing from the Usenix association of Unix users. In 1990 it launched AlterNet, one of the first commercial internet backbones, selling direct connections to companies at a time when the government-funded NSFNET barred most business traffic. In 1995 it went public, and Microsoft hired it to build the dial-up network for its new MSN service. In 1996 MFS Communications bought UUNET for about $2 billion, and within months WorldCom bought MFS. In 2006 UUNET's network passed to Verizon along with the rest of WorldCom, by then renamed MCI.`,facts:[`MFS paid about $2 billion for UUNET in 1996.`,`In 1995 UUNET built the dial-up network for Microsoft's MSN.`]},"company:akamaybe":{intro:`Modelled on Akamai Technologies, a company founded by the MIT mathematician Tom Leighton and his graduate student Danny Lewin to stop the web from grinding to a halt under its own popularity. Its name is a Hawaiian word for clever, and its idea was to keep copies of popular content on servers spread around the world, close to the people asking for it.`,how:`Akamai placed servers inside many internet providers' networks and used clever mathematics to send each visitor to a nearby, lightly loaded copy of a website's pictures, videos and files. Websites paid Akamai for the amount of data delivered, so that a sudden rush of visitors would not crash their own servers or clog the long routes across the internet.`,history:`Tom Leighton and Danny Lewin founded Akamai in 1998, after entering MIT's business plan competition with their ideas for spreading web traffic. In 1999 it began commercial service, and when it went public in October 1999 its shares rose more than fivefold on the first day. On 11 September 2001 Danny Lewin was killed aboard American Airlines Flight 11, the first plane to hit the World Trade Center. By late 2001 its shares had fallen more than 90 per cent from their peak, as dot-com customers went bust. By 2015 Akamai said its servers delivered between 15 and 30 per cent of all web traffic.`,facts:[`Akamai's shares rose more than fivefold on their first day of trading in 1999.`,`By 2015 Akamai ran more than 200,000 servers in over 100 countries.`]},"company:exodust":{intro:`Modelled on Exodus Communications, a Silicon Valley company that built and ran large data centres for internet businesses. Founded by two engineers who had come to America from India, it rented floor space, power, cooling and fast internet links to companies that needed their web servers to stay online day and night.`,how:`Exodus built secure buildings full of equipment racks, backup generators and air conditioning, and connected them to many internet backbones. Customers placed their own servers there, a service called colocation, or rented servers that Exodus managed, and paid monthly for space, power and bandwidth, with staff on hand around the clock to fix and restart machines.`,history:`K B Chandrasekhar and B V Jagadeesh founded Exodus in 1994, at first as an internet service provider. By 1996 it had turned to running data centres, starting in Santa Clara, California. It went public in 1998 and during the dot-com boom hosted many famous websites, and in 1999 Google rented space in one of its centres. At its peak in 2000 its shares valued it at about $32 billion. When the dot-com firms that rented its space went bust, Exodus could not pay its debts, and it filed for bankruptcy protection in September 2001. In 2002 the British company Cable & Wireless bought most of its data centres.`,facts:[`At its peak in 2000 Exodus was valued at about $32 billion.`,`Exodus filed for bankruptcy protection in September 2001.`]},"company:equinicks":{intro:`Modelled on Equinix, a company founded by Jay Adelson and Al Avery, two facilities managers from Digital Equipment Corporation, to build neutral meeting places for networks. Its data centres were owned by no telephone company or internet provider, so rival networks could all come together under one roof and connect directly to each other.`,how:`Networks and content companies rented cages and racks in Equinix buildings and paid for cross-connects, short cables linking them to each other inside the same building. Swapping traffic this way, called peering, was cheaper and faster than sending it across the wider internet, and the more networks gathered in one building, the more attractive it became to the next one.`,history:`Jay Adelson and Al Avery founded Equinix in 1998, drawing on their work running Digital's internet exchange in Palo Alto. Its first exchange centres opened in 1999, in Ashburn, Virginia, and Silicon Valley. It went public in 2000 and only just survived the crash that followed, merging with two Asian data centre firms in 2002. In 2015 it became a real estate investment trust, and in 2016 it completed its takeover of TelecityGroup, a big European rival. By 2024 it ran more than 260 data centres in over 70 cities around the world.`,facts:[`By 2024 Equinix ran more than 260 data centres in over 70 cities.`,`Equinix's revenue passed $8 billion in 2023.`]},"company:snackspace":{intro:`Modelled on Rackspace, a web hosting company from San Antonio, Texas, started by three Trinity University students, Richard Yoo, Dirk Elmendorf and Pat Condon. It rented out servers in its data centre to businesses that wanted a website without running their own machines, and it set out to win customers on the quality of its help.`,how:`Rackspace offered managed hosting: it bought the servers, installed them in its data centres, kept them patched and running and answered customers' calls at any hour, charging a monthly fee for each machine. Its promise of fanatical support set it apart from cheaper hosts that left customers to fend for themselves.`,history:`Richard Yoo, Dirk Elmendorf and Pat Condon founded Rackspace in 1998, backed by the local investor Graham Weston. In 2008 it went public and bought Slicehost, whose virtual servers, rented by the hour, became the basis of Rackspace Cloud Servers. In 2010 it founded the OpenStack project with NASA, offering free open-source software for building clouds. As Amazon, Microsoft and Google came to dominate cloud computing, Rackspace in 2015 began offering to manage customers' accounts on those big clouds instead. In 2016 the private equity firm Apollo bought Rackspace for $4.3 billion. In 2020 it returned to the stock market.`,facts:[`Apollo took Rackspace private for $4.3 billion in 2016.`,`OpenStack was launched with NASA in July 2010.`]},"company:cuppa":{intro:`Modelled on LEO, the Lyons Electronic Office, built by J Lyons and Co, the London company famous for its tea shops, cakes and grand Corner House restaurants. Lyons decided to build its own electronic computer to handle the huge clerical work of a catering empire, and LEO I became the first computer in the world to run routine office work.`,how:`LEO I was based on EDSAC, the Cambridge University computer, and used thousands of valves, with mercury delay lines as its memory. It worked out the orders for Lyons's bakeries, the stock for its tea shops and the wages of its staff, jobs that had kept rooms full of clerks busy. Lyons also sold time on it to other firms and government departments.`,history:`LEO I ran its first business job, valuing the output of Lyons's bakeries, in November 1951. In 1954 it began running the company's payroll, and Lyons set up LEO Computers Ltd to build machines for other customers. LEO II followed in 1957 and LEO III in 1962, sold to British firms and government departments, with a few exported to South Africa and Czechoslovakia. In 1963 LEO Computers merged with the computer arm of English Electric. In 1968 the business became part of ICL, Britain's national computer company. The last working LEO III was switched off by the Post Office in 1981.`,facts:[`LEO I ran the world's first regular office computer job in November 1951.`,`About 60 LEO III computers were built from 1962.`]},"company:dough":{intro:`Modelled on ADP, Automatic Data Processing, which began as Automatic Payrolls, a small company in Paterson, New Jersey, started by Henry Taub when he was 22. Firms sent in their workers' time records, and Automatic Payrolls worked out the pay, wrote the cheques and kept the records, so the customer did not have to.`,how:`Payroll is tedious and must be right every week: hours, tax and deductions for every worker. Automatic Payrolls charged a fee per employee to take over the job, at first with bookkeeping machines and punched-card equipment. It lived on steady, repeating income from customers who rarely switched once their payroll ran smoothly.`,history:`Henry Taub founded Automatic Payrolls in Paterson, New Jersey, in 1949, and his brother Joe and Frank Lautenberg soon joined him. In 1958 the company changed its name to Automatic Data Processing. In 1961 it went public and bought its first computer. Frank Lautenberg, by then its chief executive, left in 1982 to become a US senator. In 2006 ADP began publishing a monthly report on private-sector jobs in America, watched closely by investors. In 2014 it spun off its software business for car dealers as CDK Global.`,facts:[`Henry Taub founded the company in 1949 at the age of 22.`,`By 2023 ADP paid more than 40 million workers around the world.`]},"company:eccentric":{intro:`Modelled on EDS, Electronic Data Systems, the Dallas company that ran other organisations' computers for them, and whose founder, Ross Perot, ran it with the discipline of a navy ship. Staff wore dark suits and white shirts, kept their hair short and beards off, and were expected to work whatever hours a job required.`,how:`EDS recruited bright young people, many of them former soldiers, sailors and pilots, trained them in its own programmes and placed them in teams that took over a customer's computer room. It promised to keep the systems running and costs down under fixed long-term contracts, and its staff's loyalty and hard work were its main selling points.`,history:`Ross Perot founded EDS in Dallas in 1962. In 1979, when two EDS managers were jailed in Iran during the revolution, Perot sent a team of employees led by a retired army colonel, Arthur Simons, which got them out of the country. In 1983 the novelist Ken Follett told the story in the book On Wings of Eagles. In 1984 General Motors bought EDS, and in 1988 Perot founded a rival, Perot Systems, after GM bought him out. In 2000 EDS ran a Super Bowl advert showing cowboys herding cats, a joke about managing complex computer systems. In 2008 Hewlett-Packard bought EDS.`,facts:[`EDS employed about 140,000 people when Hewlett-Packard bought it in 2008.`,`Ross Perot won about 19 per cent of the vote for US president in 1992.`]},"company:thymeshare":{intro:`Modelled on Tymshare, the Cupertino timesharing company that sold computer time by the minute to customers who dialled in from terminals. Its programmers turned its machines into busy shared services, and they faced a problem: customers far away had to pay for long-distance calls to reach them.`,how:`Tymshare's answer was its own network, Tymnet, built from small minicomputers placed in cities across America. A customer made a cheap local call to the nearest node, and the network carried the typed characters to whichever Tymshare computer had room, choosing the route from a central supervisor program. Customers paid for computing, and the network was paid for within that price.`,history:`Tymshare grew from its founding in 1964 into a large timesharing firm, and in 1966 it began running SDS 940 computers. In 1968 two of its engineers, Norm Hardy and LaRoy Tymes, proposed a network of minicomputers to link terminals to its computers. In November 1971 Tymnet's central supervisor went into service, and the network soon reached cities across America. In 1977 Tymshare bought NLS, Douglas Engelbart's pioneering office system, from the Stanford Research Institute and sold it as Augment. In 1984 McDonnell Douglas bought Tymshare, and in 1989 it sold Tymnet to British Telecom.`,facts:[`The Tymnet supervisor, written by LaRoy Tymes, went into service in November 1971.`,`British Telecom bought Tymnet from McDonnell Douglas in 1989.`]},"company:sapsucker":{intro:`Modelled on SAP, a German software company founded by five former IBM engineers, among them Dietmar Hopp and Hasso Plattner. Its first name, Systemanalyse Programmentwicklung, meant systems analysis and program development, and its idea was standard business software: one ready-made program for accounts and other office work that many companies could buy, instead of each writing its own.`,how:`SAP's software handled the books, purchasing, stock and sales in one system, processing each transaction as it happened rather than in overnight batches, which its founders called real time. It sold licences and charged a yearly fee for support and updates, and customers often changed their own ways of working to fit its built-in methods.`,history:`Dietmar Hopp, Hasso Plattner, Claus Wellenreuther, Klaus Tschira and Hans-Werner Hector set up SAP in Weinheim in 1972. Its first customer was the German arm of ICI, the chemicals firm, and in 1973 it finished its first financial accounting program. In 1979 it launched R/2 for mainframes, which made it a leader among big European companies. In 1992 it launched R/3, a client-server version for cheaper Unix computers, and it spread to big companies around the world. In 2015 it launched S/4HANA, a new suite built on its own fast in-memory database.`,facts:[`SAP finished its first accounting program, RF, in 1973.`,`SAP employed more than 100,000 people by 2021.`]},"company:peoplesofa":{intro:`Modelled on PeopleSoft, a business software company from the San Francisco Bay Area founded by Dave Duffield and Ken Morris to sell programs for human resources: the records of a company's staff, their pay, benefits and careers. It aimed at firms moving from mainframes to cheaper networks of smaller computers, and it was known for its friendly, relaxed workplace.`,how:`PeopleSoft sold licences for its human resources software, then for payroll, finance and other modules, and charged yearly fees for support and updates. Its programs used a client-server design, with staff working on desktop PCs connected to a central database, and customers could adapt the screens and rules with PeopleSoft's own development tools.`,history:`Dave Duffield and Ken Morris founded PeopleSoft in 1987. It went public in 1992 and grew fast as large companies replaced their old mainframe systems. By 1998 its yearly revenue had passed $1 billion. In June 2003 it agreed to buy JD Edwards, a rival, and days later Oracle launched a hostile bid for PeopleSoft itself. In October 2004 the board fired chief executive Craig Conway and brought Duffield back, and in December 2004 PeopleSoft accepted Oracle's offer of about $10.3 billion. The deal closed in January 2005, and Oracle cut thousands of PeopleSoft jobs.`,facts:[`PeopleSoft's revenue passed $1 billion in 1998.`,`Oracle paid about $10.3 billion for PeopleSoft after an 18-month fight, agreed in December 2004.`]},"company:baaan":{intro:`Modelled on Baan, a Dutch business software company founded by Jan Baan in the small town of Barneveld. It began by offering bookkeeping advice and writing accounting programs for local firms, a family business in the Dutch countryside that set its sights on manufacturers.`,how:`Baan wrote software that tracked a manufacturer's orders, stock, production schedules and accounts in one system, and sold it with licence and support fees. Its programs ran on Unix computers, and it won customers with flexible tools that let a firm model its own way of working rather than bending to the software.`,history:`Jan Baan started the company in Barneveld in 1978. In 1994 Boeing chose Baan's software to run its aircraft factories, a big win that helped the company go public in 1995. By 1997 it was one of the five biggest makers of business planning software in the world. In 1998 it admitted that it had inflated its sales, partly by booking licences sold to firms linked to the Baan family, and its shares collapsed. In 2000 the British engineering group Invensys bought Baan for about $700 million. Invensys sold it in 2003 to SSA Global, which was bought by Infor in 2006.`,facts:[`Boeing chose Baan's software for its factories in 1994.`,`Invensys bought the troubled Baan for about $700 million in 2000.`]},"company:sibling":{intro:`Modelled on Siebel Systems, a software company from San Mateo, California, founded by Tom Siebel, a former sales executive at Oracle, with Pat House. It sold customer relationship management software: programs that track a company's dealings with its customers, such as sales leads, contacts, deals and service calls.`,how:`Siebel began with software for salespeople, who kept their contacts and forecasts on laptops and synchronised them with a central database, and it added modules for marketing and customer service. It sold expensive licences for each user, often for whole sales forces at once, and charged for the support and consulting needed to install it.`,history:`Tom Siebel and Pat House founded Siebel Systems in 1993. It went public in 1996 and grew at a tremendous pace, and in 2000 its revenue reached about $1.8 billion. When the dot-com bust hit in 2001, sales fell, and new rivals selling software over the web, such as Salesforce, attacked its market. In 2006 Oracle completed its purchase of Siebel for about $5.85 billion. In 2009 Tom Siebel founded C3, a company selling artificial intelligence software to big businesses.`,facts:[`Siebel's revenue reached about $1.8 billion in 2000.`,`Oracle bought Siebel for about $5.85 billion, closing the deal in January 2006.`]},"company:salesfarce":{intro:`Modelled on Salesforce, a San Francisco company founded by Marc Benioff, a former Oracle executive, to sell customer relationship management software over the web. Customers installed nothing: they opened a web browser, logged in and paid a monthly fee for each user, a model it promoted with the slogan No Software.`,how:`Salesforce ran one copy of its software on its own servers for all its customers, keeping each firm's data apart, so that everyone received updates at the same time. This model, called software as a service, let small firms start cheaply, and it made the company's income steady and predictable, because customers renewed subscriptions instead of buying a licence once.`,history:`Marc Benioff founded Salesforce in a San Francisco flat in March 1999 with Parker Harris and two other engineers. In 2000 it staged a mock protest outside a big rival's conference, with hired actors waving No Software signs. It went public in 2004, and in 2006 it opened AppExchange, an online store for add-ons built by other firms. In 2019 it bought Tableau, a maker of data charts, for about $15.7 billion. In 2021 it completed its largest takeover, buying the workplace messaging company Slack for about $27.7 billion.`,facts:[`Salesforce paid about $27.7 billion for Slack in 2021.`,`Its revenue was about $34.9 billion in the year to January 2024.`]},"company:netsweet":{intro:`Modelled on NetSuite, which began as NetLedger, a company from San Mateo, California, founded by Evan Goldberg with money from Oracle's founder Larry Ellison. It offered accounting software for small businesses that ran entirely on the web, so firms could keep their books in a browser without buying servers or installing programs.`,how:`NetLedger hosted its software on its own servers and charged customers a monthly subscription. It added stock control, orders, customer records and online shop tools to the accounts, so that a growing company could run its whole back office in one system. It aimed at firms too small for the big packages of SAP or Oracle.`,history:`Evan Goldberg founded NetLedger in 1998, backed by Larry Ellison. In July 2002 Zach Nelson, a former marketing chief at Network Associates, became chief executive. In 2003 the company renamed itself NetSuite, after its widening range of software. It went public on the New York Stock Exchange in December 2007. In November 2016 Oracle completed its purchase of NetSuite for about $9.3 billion, buying a company its own founder had helped to start.`,facts:[`NetSuite went public in December 2007.`,`Oracle paid about $9.3 billion for NetSuite in 2016.`]},"company:workdaze":{intro:`Modelled on Workday, a business software company from Pleasanton, California, founded by Dave Duffield, the founder of PeopleSoft, with Aneel Bhusri after Oracle had taken PeopleSoft over. It set out to rebuild human resources and finance software from scratch to run in the cloud, sold as a subscription over the web.`,how:`Workday ran its software on its own servers for all its customers at once, delivering updates to everyone a few times a year, and charged yearly subscriptions based on the number of a customer's employees. Its human resources system kept staff records, pay, benefits and hiring in one place, with finance and planning software beside it.`,history:`Dave Duffield and Aneel Bhusri founded Workday in March 2005. It went public in October 2012, and its shares rose about 74 per cent on the first day of trading. In 2018 it bought Adaptive Insights, a maker of planning software, for about $1.55 billion. By 2024 its yearly revenue had passed $7 billion, and many of the largest American companies ran their human resources on it.`,facts:[`Workday's shares jumped about 74 per cent on their first day of trading in 2012.`,`Workday bought Adaptive Insights for about $1.55 billion in 2018.`]},"company:twang":{intro:`Modelled on Wang Laboratories, the company founded in Boston by An Wang, a Chinese-born physicist from Harvard who had helped invent magnetic core memory, a way of storing computer data in tiny rings of magnetic material. It began as a one-man business making and selling electronic devices, such as memory cores and counters, to other firms.`,how:`Wang began by selling magnetic core memories and custom electronics to other manufacturers, and An Wang held a valuable patent on core memory. The firm's strength was clever, compact electronics packaged so that people who were not engineers could use them, and it sold mainly to businesses and laboratories.`,history:`An Wang founded Wang Laboratories in Boston in 1951. In 1956 IBM bought his core memory patent for $500,000. In 1965 its electronic desk calculators made it a fast-growing company. In 1976 it launched a word processing system in which several typists shared one computer through their own screens, and it quickly became the leading maker of office word processors. In 1986 An Wang made his son Fred president, and as cheap personal computers with word processing programs replaced Wang's machines, losses mounted. An Wang died in 1990, and in August 1992 Wang Laboratories filed for bankruptcy protection.`,facts:[`IBM paid $500,000 for An Wang's core memory patent in 1956.`,`Wang's sales reached about $3 billion a year at their peak in the late 1980s.`]},"company:microamateur":{intro:`Modelled on MicroPro International, a software company from San Rafael, California, founded by Seymour Rubinstein, a former salesman for the computer maker IMSAI, to sell programs for the small computers that ran the CP/M operating system. Its star was WordStar, a word processor that let ordinary microcomputers do work that had needed expensive dedicated office machines.`,how:`WordStar was written in assembly language by Rob Barnaby and controlled entirely from the keyboard, with commands such as Ctrl-K B to mark the start of a block of text, so typists never lifted their hands. It showed line breaks and pages on screen as they would print. MicroPro sold it through computer dealers and had it bundled with new machines.`,history:`Seymour Rubinstein founded MicroPro in 1978, and WordStar, written by Rob Barnaby, went on sale in 1979. In 1981 it came free with the Osborne 1 portable computer, which helped make it the most popular word processor of its time. In 1984 MicroPro went public and launched WordStar 2000, which dropped the familiar commands, and many users moved to WordPerfect. In 1989 the company renamed itself WordStar International. In 1994 it merged into SoftKey. In 2014 the novelist George R R Martin said he still wrote his books with WordStar 4.0 on an old DOS computer.`,facts:[`WordStar went on sale for CP/M computers in 1979.`,`The Osborne 1 of 1981 came bundled with WordStar.`]},"company:softsmarts":{intro:`Modelled on Software Arts, a company in Cambridge, Massachusetts, founded by Dan Bricklin and Bob Frankston, two MIT graduates, to write VisiCalc, the first electronic spreadsheet for personal computers. Bricklin, a business school student, imagined a calculator with a screen like a blackboard, where changing one number would update every total that depended on it.`,how:`Software Arts wrote the programs, while a separate publisher, Personal Software, marketed them and paid the authors royalties. VisiCalc showed a grid of rows and columns; users typed numbers and formulas into the cells, and the program recalculated everything at once. Accountants and managers bought Apple II computers just to run it.`,history:`Dan Bricklin and Bob Frankston founded Software Arts in January 1979, and VisiCalc went on sale for the Apple II in October 1979 for about $100. In 1981 versions for other computers followed, including the new IBM PC. Bricklin had not patented the idea, since software patents were rarely granted in 1979, and rivals copied it freely. In 1983 Software Arts and its publisher, by then called VisiCorp, sued each other, just as Lotus 1-2-3 was taking over the market. In 1985 Lotus bought Software Arts and soon stopped selling VisiCalc.`,facts:[`VisiCalc cost about $100 when it went on sale in 1979.`,`Lotus bought Software Arts in 1985 and dropped VisiCalc soon after.`]},"company:lotion":{intro:`Modelled on Lotus Development, a software company in Cambridge, Massachusetts, founded by Mitch Kapor, a former disc jockey and meditation teacher who had worked for VisiCalc's publisher, with the programmer Jonathan Sachs. Its program, Lotus 1-2-3, combined a spreadsheet, charts and a simple database in one package for the IBM PC.`,how:`Lotus 1-2-3 was written in fast assembly language for the IBM PC and used its memory and screen to the full, so it ran far more quickly than rivals adapted from older machines. Lotus spent heavily on advertising, sold through dealers for $495 a copy and made huge margins, because each disk and manual cost little to make.`,history:`Mitch Kapor and Jonathan Sachs founded Lotus in 1982, and 1-2-3 went on sale in January 1983. In 1983 Lotus took in $53 million, as 1-2-3 became the best-selling program of its time and a main reason businesses bought IBM PCs. In 1989 it released Notes, a system designed by Ray Ozzie for sharing documents, databases and messages across a company. By 1993 Microsoft Excel, running on Windows, was outselling 1-2-3. In 1995 IBM bought Lotus for about $3.5 billion, chiefly for Notes.`,facts:[`Lotus took in $53 million in 1983, its first year of sales.`,`Lotus 1-2-3 sold for $495 a copy in 1983.`,`IBM paid about $3.5 billion for Lotus in 1995.`]},"company:wordimperfect":{intro:`Modelled on WordPerfect Corporation, which began as Satellite Software International in Orem, Utah, founded by Alan Ashton, a computer science professor at Brigham Young University, and his student Bruce Bastian. They wrote a word processing program for the city of Orem's Data General minicomputer and then sold it to others.`,how:`WordPerfect kept the screen almost clear of menus and used the function keys for commands, with a cardboard template laid over the keyboard to remind users which key did what. Its reveal codes view showed the hidden formatting marks in a document. The company offered free, unlimited telephone help, and its huge support centre became a selling point.`,history:`Alan Ashton and Bruce Bastian founded Satellite Software International in 1979. In 1982 it released WordPerfect for the IBM PC, and in 1986 the company took the WordPerfect name. WordPerfect 5.1 for DOS, released in 1989, became the most popular word processor in the world, especially in law firms and offices. Its slow and troubled move to Windows let Microsoft Word take the lead, and in 1994 the network software company Novell bought WordPerfect. In 1996 Novell sold it on to Corel for a fraction of what it had paid.`,facts:[`WordPerfect 5.1 for DOS appeared in 1989 and became the office standard.`,`Novell bought WordPerfect in 1994 and sold it to Corel in 1996.`]},"company:aldente":{intro:`Modelled on Aldus Corporation, a Seattle software company founded by Paul Brainerd, a former newspaper editor, and named after Aldus Manutius, a Venetian printer of the Renaissance. It made PageMaker, a program for laying out pages of text and pictures on a personal computer, ready to print.`,how:`PageMaker showed a page on screen as it would print, and users placed columns of text and pictures on it with the mouse, much as a designer had done with paper and glue. Combined with the Apple Macintosh and a laser printer that understood the PostScript page language, it let small offices make newsletters and brochures that once needed a typesetter.`,history:`Paul Brainerd founded Aldus in Seattle in 1984 and coined the phrase desktop publishing. PageMaker for the Macintosh went on sale in July 1985, and with Apple's LaserWriter printer it started a desktop publishing boom. In 1987 a version for Windows followed. In 1987 QuarkXPress appeared, and it soon overtook PageMaker among professional designers. In 1994 Aldus merged into Adobe Systems in a deal worth about $525 million.`,facts:[`PageMaker 1.0 for the Macintosh cost $495 in 1985.`,`Adobe's merger with Aldus in 1994 was worth about $525 million.`]},"company:aoffline":{intro:`Modelled on AOL, America Online, a company from Vienna, Virginia, built by Steve Case, Jim Kimsey and Marc Seriff out of the remains of a failed business that sent video games down telephone lines. It ran online services that home computer users reached by modem, where they could chat, swap messages, play games and send e-mail.`,how:`Rather than sell computer time to experts, the company built easy screens and friendly guides for families, and it ran online services for computer makers such as Commodore and Apple under their names. Its chat rooms and its cheerful "You've got mail" greeting made being online feel sociable, and it earned money from membership fees.`,history:`In 1983 Control Video Corporation launched GameLine, which sent games to Atari 2600 consoles over the phone, and when it failed its remains became Quantum Computer Services in 1985. In 1989 the actor Elwood Edwards recorded its famous "Welcome" and "You've got mail" greetings. In 1991 the company renamed itself America Online, and Steve Case became chief executive. In 1993 AOL opened a gateway to the Usenet discussion groups, and the flood of newcomers became known as the Eternal September. In 1997 it launched AOL Instant Messenger, and in 1999 it bought Netscape. In 2015 Verizon bought AOL for $4.4 billion, and in 2025 the Italian app company Bending Spoons agreed to buy it from Yahoo.`,facts:[`AOL bought the web browser company Netscape in 1999 for shares worth about $10 billion.`,`Verizon paid $4.4 billion for AOL in 2015.`,`Bending Spoons agreed in 2025 to buy AOL for about $1.5 billion.`]},"company:netscrape":{intro:`Modelled on Netscape Communications, a company from Mountain View, California, founded by Jim Clark, the founder of Silicon Graphics, and Marc Andreessen, a young programmer who had co-written the Mosaic web browser at the University of Illinois. Its browser, Netscape Navigator, made the World Wide Web quick and easy to use on ordinary computers.`,how:`Netscape let students and others download Navigator free, while asking businesses to pay, and it earned more from selling web server software to companies building websites. It added new features faster than standards bodies could agree them, such as secure connections for shopping online and a language for making web pages interactive.`,history:`Jim Clark and Marc Andreessen founded Mosaic Communications in April 1994, renamed it Netscape that November and released Navigator in December 1994. Its share sale in August 1995 was a sensation: the shares, offered at $28, opened at $71, valuing a company with almost no profit at nearly $3 billion. In 1995 its programmer Brendan Eich wrote JavaScript in about ten days. Microsoft fought back by giving Internet Explorer away with Windows, and in 1998 Netscape released its browser's source code, starting the Mozilla project. In 1999 AOL bought Netscape for shares worth about $10 billion.`,facts:[`AOL's purchase of Netscape was agreed in November 1998 at about $4.2 billion.`,`Roughly 80 per cent of web users browsed with Navigator in 1995.`]},"company:yawhoo":{intro:`Modelled on Yahoo!, which began as a list of favourite websites kept by two Stanford University electrical engineering students, Jerry Yang and David Filo. They sorted the young web's sites into categories by hand, and their directory grew so popular that they turned it into a company.`,how:`Yahoo was a portal: a front page to the web where people could browse the directory, search, and read news, stock prices and sports scores, all paid for by advertising. Its editors, called surfers, reviewed websites and filed them under topics, and Yahoo sold banner adverts seen by the millions who began their time online there.`,history:`Jerry Yang and David Filo started their list in January 1994 as Jerry and David's Guide to the World Wide Web and renamed it Yahoo! that spring. They formed a company in March 1995 with money from Sequoia Capital, and it went public in April 1996. In January 2000 its shares valued it at about $125 billion. In 2008 it turned down a takeover offer from Microsoft worth about $44.6 billion. In 2016 and 2017 it revealed data breaches that had affected all of its 3 billion accounts. In 2017 Verizon bought Yahoo's main business for about $4.5 billion.`,facts:[`Yahoo was worth about $125 billion at its peak in January 2000.`,`Microsoft offered about $44.6 billion for Yahoo in 2008.`,`Verizon paid about $4.5 billion for Yahoo's main business in 2017.`]},"company:hastalavista":{intro:`Modelled on AltaVista, a web search engine built at Digital Equipment Corporation's research laboratory in Palo Alto, California, partly to show off the power of Digital's new 64-bit Alpha computers. It aimed to index the full text of every page on the web, so that anyone could find any word on any page in a fraction of a second.`,how:`A crawler program called Scooter fetched millions of web pages, and the index lived on big Alpha servers that answered questions almost instantly. Unlike directories edited by people, AltaVista searched the whole text of pages and let users look for exact phrases. It was free to use, paid for by advertising and by the publicity it brought Digital's machines.`,history:`Louis Monier, Michael Burrows and Paul Flaherty built AltaVista at Digital, and it opened to the public in December 1995. In 1996 it supplied the search results behind Yahoo's directory. In December 1997 it launched Babel Fish, a free service built with Systran's software that translated web pages. In 1998 Compaq bought Digital and with it AltaVista, and in 1999 it sold most of it to the internet company CMGI for about $2.3 billion. Google's better results drew its users away, and in 2003 Overture bought AltaVista for about $140 million, before Yahoo bought Overture that same year. Yahoo closed AltaVista in July 2013.`,facts:[`CMGI paid about $2.3 billion for most of AltaVista in 1999.`,`Overture bought AltaVista for about $140 million in 2003.`,`Yahoo shut AltaVista down in July 2013.`]},"company:overtired":{intro:`Modelled on GoTo.com, a search engine from Pasadena, California, created at Bill Gross's Idealab, a business that started new internet companies. Its idea was to let advertisers bid for the top places in its search results for each word, paying only when someone clicked through to their site.`,how:`Advertisers chose search words, such as flowers, and named the price they would pay for each click; the highest bidder appeared first in the results. GoTo earned money every time a searcher clicked, and it could also feed its paid listings to other websites in return for a share of the money, which made it a wholesaler of searches.`,history:`Bill Gross's Idealab set up GoTo.com in 1997, and it launched its pay-per-click search in February 1998. It went public in 1999, and by 2000 it was supplying paid listings to big portals such as AOL. In 2001 it renamed itself Overture Services and stopped running its own search site to concentrate on its partners, chiefly Yahoo and Microsoft's MSN. In 2002 it sued Google, claiming its AdWords system infringed a patent on bid-ranked results. In 2003 Yahoo bought Overture for about $1.63 billion. In 2004 Google settled the patent case by giving Yahoo 2.7 million of its shares.`,facts:[`Yahoo paid about $1.63 billion for Overture in 2003.`,`Google gave Yahoo 2.7 million shares to settle the Overture patent suit in 2004.`]},"company:doubleclique":{intro:`Modelled on DoubleClick, a New York company founded by Kevin O'Connor and Dwight Merriman to place banner adverts on websites. It served adverts from its own computers across a network of sites, tracking which ones people saw and clicked, so that advertisers could aim their messages and measure the results.`,how:`When someone opened a page in DoubleClick's network, the advert came from DoubleClick's servers, which used a small file stored in the browser, called a cookie, to recognise the same visitor across many sites. That let it limit how often each person saw an advert, count clicks and target likely buyers, and it took a share of what advertisers paid.`,history:`Kevin O'Connor and Dwight Merriman founded DoubleClick in 1996, building on an online advertising unit of the agency Poppe Tyson. It went public in 1998, and in 1999 it bought Abacus Direct, a database of catalogue shoppers, for about $1 billion. Its plan to link people's names and addresses with their web browsing caused a privacy outcry and a government inquiry in 2000, and it backed down. In 2005 the private equity firm Hellman & Friedman bought DoubleClick for about $1.1 billion. In 2008 Google bought it for $3.1 billion after regulators in America and Europe approved the deal.`,facts:[`DoubleClick bought Abacus Direct for about $1 billion in 1999.`,`Google paid $3.1 billion for DoubleClick in 2008.`]},"company:fleabay":{intro:`Modelled on eBay, which began as AuctionWeb, a page on the personal website of Pierre Omidyar, a software engineer in San Jose, California, where anyone could put an item up for auction. Buyers bid against each other online, and whoever had the highest bid when the auction closed won the item.`,how:`eBay held no stock and shipped nothing: sellers listed items, buyers bid, and the two arranged payment and postage between themselves. It charged sellers a small listing fee and a cut of the final price. A feedback system, in which buyers and sellers rated each other after every sale, helped strangers trust one another.`,history:`Pierre Omidyar launched AuctionWeb in September 1995, and one of the first items sold was a broken laser pointer, for $14.83. In 1997 the site took the name eBay, from Omidyar's consulting firm, Echo Bay Technology Group. Meg Whitman became chief executive in 1998, and the company went public that September. In 2002 it bought PayPal for $1.5 billion, and in 2005 it bought the internet telephone company Skype for $2.6 billion. In 2015 it split off PayPal as a separate company again.`,facts:[`A broken laser pointer sold on AuctionWeb for $14.83 in 1995.`,`eBay paid $2.6 billion for Skype in 2005.`,`eBay had about 130 million active buyers in 2023.`]},"company:payperil":{intro:`Modelled on PayPal, which began as Confinity, a Palo Alto start-up founded by Max Levchin, Peter Thiel and Luke Nosek to beam money between Palm Pilot handheld computers. Its lasting idea was to let anyone send money to anyone else over the internet, using nothing more than an e-mail address.`,how:`Users opened an account linked to a credit card or bank account, and payments moved between accounts at once, so a buyer on an auction site could pay a stranger without posting a cheque. PayPal earned a fee on payments to businesses, and it fought a flood of fraud with its own detection software.`,history:`Max Levchin, Peter Thiel and Luke Nosek founded Confinity in December 1998, and in 1999 it launched PayPal. In March 2000 Confinity merged with X.com, the online bank started by Elon Musk, and in 2001 the company took the PayPal name. It spread fast among eBay sellers and went public in February 2002. In October 2002 eBay bought PayPal for $1.5 billion. By 2006 former PayPal staff, nicknamed the PayPal Mafia, had founded or funded YouTube, LinkedIn, Yelp and Tesla. In July 2015 eBay spun PayPal off as an independent company.`,facts:[`eBay paid $1.5 billion for PayPal in 2002.`,`PayPal had about 430 million active accounts in 2023.`]},"company:fiendster":{intro:`Modelled on Friendster, a social networking website from Mountain View, California, created by Jonathan Abrams, a Canadian programmer, as a way to meet new people through friends of friends. Members made a profile with a photo, linked to their friends and browsed the wider web of people connected to them.`,how:`Friendster showed each member the people up to four steps away in their web of friends, and it worked out these connections afresh every time a page loaded, which put a crushing load on its servers. It was free to use and hoped to earn money from advertising and paid extras, once it had enough members.`,history:`Jonathan Abrams launched Friendster in 2002, and within months of opening widely in 2003 it had about 3 million members. In 2003 Google offered to buy it for about $30 million in shares, and Abrams turned the offer down. Pages took ages to load, the site deleted joke profiles that members loved, and in 2004 many American users moved to MySpace. Friendster stayed popular in South-East Asia, and in 2009 the Malaysian company MOL Global bought it. In 2011 it relaunched as a games site, and in 2015 it shut down.`,facts:[`Google offered about $30 million for Friendster in 2003.`,`Friendster had about 3 million members within months of opening widely in 2003.`]},"company:mespace":{intro:`Modelled on MySpace, a social networking website from Santa Monica, California, started by staff of the internet marketing firm eUniverse, led by Chris DeWolfe and Tom Anderson. Members built colourful personal pages, ranked their top friends and shared music, especially from bands that put their songs on the site.`,how:`Every new member's first friend was Tom, the co-founder, and members could change the look of their pages with their own code for colours, pictures and songs. Unsigned bands used MySpace to reach fans for free, and the site made its money from advertising shown to its young audience.`,history:`Chris DeWolfe and Tom Anderson launched MySpace in August 2003. In July 2005 Rupert Murdoch's News Corporation bought its parent company, Intermix Media, for $580 million. In 2006 it was for a time the most visited website in the United States, and that year it signed a $900 million advertising deal with Google. In 2008 Facebook passed it in visitors around the world, and its members drifted away. In 2011 News Corp sold MySpace to the advertising firm Specific Media, with the singer Justin Timberlake as a part-owner, for about $35 million. In 2019 the site admitted it had lost the music uploaded during its first 12 years in a server move.`,facts:[`News Corp bought MySpace's parent company for $580 million in 2005.`,`MySpace was sold for about $35 million in 2011.`,`In 2019 MySpace admitted losing about 50 million songs uploaded from 2003 to 2015.`]},"company:linkedout":{intro:`Modelled on LinkedIn, a social network for working life founded in Reid Hoffman's living room in Mountain View, California, with four co-founders. Members posted their career history like an online CV and connected with colleagues, so that they could find jobs, staff and business contacts through people they already knew.`,how:`LinkedIn was free to join and made money from recruiters who paid to search for and contact members, from premium accounts and from advertising. Its value grew with every member, because people joined to be found by employers and to stay in touch with former colleagues, and employers came because the people were there.`,history:`Reid Hoffman founded LinkedIn in December 2002 and opened the site in May 2003. In 2008 Jeff Weiner, a former Yahoo executive, joined, and in 2009 he became chief executive. It went public in May 2011, and its shares more than doubled on the first day. In 2016 Microsoft bought LinkedIn for about $26.2 billion, then its largest purchase ever. By 2024 it had more than a billion registered members.`,facts:[`LinkedIn's shares more than doubled on their first day of trading in 2011.`,`Microsoft paid about $26.2 billion for LinkedIn in 2016.`,`LinkedIn passed a billion registered members by 2024.`]},"company:jitter":{intro:`Modelled on Twitter, a San Francisco service where people posted short public messages, called tweets, of up to 140 characters, the length of a mobile phone text message with room for a name. It grew out of Odeo, a podcasting company, where Jack Dorsey, Evan Williams, Biz Stone and Noah Glass dreamed it up as a way to share what you were doing.`,how:`Users followed other people's accounts and saw their tweets in a timeline, and anyone could reply, repost a tweet or tag a topic. The service was free and planned to make money from advertising, and its open, public stream made it a place where news, jokes and rumours spread within minutes.`,history:`Jack Dorsey sent the first tweet on 21 March 2006, and the service opened to the public that July. It took off at the South by Southwest festival in Austin in 2007, where big screens showed tweets as they were sent. In 2009 a passenger's photo of a plane that had landed on the Hudson River in New York reached Twitter before any news channel. It went public in November 2013, and in 2017 it doubled the limit to 280 characters. In October 2022 Elon Musk bought Twitter for about $44 billion, and in 2023 he renamed it X.`,facts:[`Jack Dorsey's first tweet in 2006 read "just setting up my twttr".`,`The 140-character limit doubled to 280 in 2017.`,`Elon Musk paid about $44 billion for Twitter in 2022.`]},"company:netflux":{intro:`Modelled on Netflix, founded in Scotts Valley, California, by Reed Hastings and Marc Randolph to rent films on DVD by post. Customers chose titles on its website, and the discs arrived in red envelopes, to be posted back when watched; DVDs were new, light and cheap to mail, unlike bulky videotapes.`,how:`Netflix kept a large stock of DVDs in warehouses near big cities, so most discs arrived the day after they were posted. At first it charged for each rental, and then it moved to a monthly subscription with no late fees and no due dates, letting members keep a queue of films that were sent out as each one came back.`,history:`Reed Hastings and Marc Randolph founded Netflix in August 1997, and its website opened in April 1998 with about 925 titles. In 1999 it switched to a monthly subscription. In 2000 it offered to sell itself to Blockbuster, the video rental chain, for $50 million, and Blockbuster turned it down. It went public in 2002, and in 2007 it began streaming films over the internet to members' computers. In 2010 Blockbuster filed for bankruptcy. In 2013 Netflix released its first big original series, House of Cards, and in 2016 it opened in almost every country in the world.`,facts:[`The Netflix website opened in 1998 with about 925 titles.`,`Blockbuster turned down the chance to buy Netflix for $50 million in 2000.`,`Netflix had more than 300 million paying members by the end of 2024.`]},"company:metube":{intro:`Modelled on YouTube, a video-sharing website founded in San Mateo, California, by Chad Hurley, Steve Chen and Jawed Karim, three former PayPal employees. Anyone could upload a video and share it with a link, and the site played it straight in the web browser, which made posting and watching clips easy for everyone.`,how:`YouTube converted every upload into a format that played in the Flash plug-in found on most computers, and it let other websites embed its videos for free, which spread them across blogs and social networks. It was free to watch, to be paid for by advertising, and it faced huge bills for bandwidth and complaints from film and music companies about copied clips.`,history:`Jawed Karim uploaded the first video, Me at the zoo, in April 2005, and the site opened fully in December 2005. In November 2006 Google bought YouTube for $1.65 billion in shares. In 2007 it began sharing advertising money with popular video makers, and that year the media company Viacom sued it for $1 billion over copied programmes, a case settled in 2014. In December 2012 the music video Gangnam Style became the first to pass a billion views. By 2024 YouTube's advertising was earning about $36 billion a year.`,facts:[`Google paid $1.65 billion for YouTube in 2006.`,`Gangnam Style passed a billion views in December 2012.`,`YouTube's adverts earned about $36 billion in 2024.`]},"company:magnabox":{intro:`Modelled on Magnavox, an American electronics company whose name is Latin for great voice. It grew from the work of two engineers in California, Peter Jensen and Edwin Pridham, who invented the moving-coil loudspeaker, a device that turned weak electrical signals into loud, clear sound.`,how:`Magnavox made loudspeakers and public address systems, at first so speakers could address large crowds outdoors, and then speakers and sets for radio listeners at home. It sold well-made home entertainment equipment through its own network of dealers, and it was quick to license useful inventions from others.`,history:`Peter Jensen and Edwin Pridham demonstrated their loudspeaker in 1915, and in 1917 the Magnavox Company was formed. In 1919 Magnavox speakers let President Woodrow Wilson address a crowd of about 50,000 people in San Diego. In 1972 it launched the Odyssey, the first home video game console, built to designs by Ralph Baer that it licensed from the defence firm Sanders Associates. In 1974 the Dutch electronics giant Philips bought Magnavox. In 1976 Atari settled a patent suit over Baer's inventions, the first of many companies to pay Magnavox for them.`,facts:[`The Odyssey of 1972 sold about 350,000 units.`,`Atari paid $700,000 in 1976 to settle Magnavox's video game patent suit.`]},"company:atarri":{intro:`Modelled on Atari, the company founded in California by Nolan Bushnell and Ted Dabney to make coin-operated video arcade games. Its name came from the Japanese board game Go, where atari is the warning that a group of stones is about to be captured. Its first big game was Pong, a simple electronic table tennis game for bars.`,how:`Atari designed arcade cabinets and sold them to the operators who placed them in bars and arcades, where players fed them coins. Its games had to be learned in seconds by someone with a drink in hand, and it was run by young engineers with a famously casual style.`,history:`Nolan Bushnell and Ted Dabney founded Atari in June 1972, and Pong, built by the engineer Allan Alcorn, was a hit that autumn. In 1976 Bushnell sold Atari to Warner Communications for about $28 million. In 1977 it launched the Video Computer System, a home console that played games on swappable cartridges, and in 1979 Warner pushed Bushnell out. In 1982 its rushed game of the film ET and its weak version of Pac-Man disappointed buyers, and in 1983 the American video game market crashed. In 1984 Warner split the company, selling the home business to Jack Tramiel as Atari Corporation and keeping the arcade side as Atari Games.`,facts:[`Warner bought Atari for about $28 million in 1976.`,`The Atari 2600 console sold about 30 million units by 1992.`,`In 1983 Atari buried unsold cartridges in a landfill in Alamogordo, New Mexico.`]},"company:mattle":{intro:`Modelled on Mattel Electronics, the electronics arm of Mattel, the Californian toy maker behind the Barbie doll. It began with small handheld games that used blips of red LED light instead of a screen, such as Auto Race and Football, sold in toy shops as a new kind of toy.`,how:`Mattel's engineers fitted calculator chips and LED displays into pocket-sized plastic cases, and the toy company sold them through its huge network of shops. Its strength over electronics firms was its marketing power and its place on toy-shop shelves, and it soon set out to take Atari on in home video games.`,history:`Mattel's handheld Auto Race appeared in 1976 and Football in 1977, and Football sold in the millions. In 1979 Mattel tested its Intellivision console in Fresno, California, and launched it across America in 1980. Its 1980 adverts, starring the writer George Plimpton, compared Intellivision's sports games side by side with Atari's. In 1983 the video game crash cost Mattel Electronics about $300 million. In January 1984 Mattel closed the division, and former staff bought the Intellivision rights and kept selling it.`,facts:[`Mattel Electronics lost about $300 million in 1983.`,`About 3 million Intellivision consoles were sold from 1980.`]},"company:cabbageco":{intro:`Modelled on Coleco, short for the Connecticut Leather Company, founded in Hartford by Maurice Greenberg, an immigrant from Russia. It sold leather, tools and supplies to shoe repairers across New England, a plain trade far removed from toys or electronics.`,how:`The company bought leather in bulk and sold it, with laces, nails and tools, to the many cobblers who mended shoes. Its strength was selling cheap, practical goods in volume to shops, and it was always ready to switch to whatever product it could make or sell in large numbers.`,history:`Maurice Greenberg founded the Connecticut Leather Company in Hartford in 1932. In the 1950s it began making leather craft kits for children and then plastic paddling pools, which became its main business. In 1976 its Telstar, a cheap console playing Pong-style games, sold about a million units. In 1982 its ColecoVision console, which came with an excellent version of Donkey Kong, was a hit. In 1983 its Adam home computer flopped, plagued by faulty tape drives, but that Christmas its Cabbage Patch Kids dolls caused near-riots in shops. In 1985 it stopped making the Adam, and in July 1988 Coleco filed for bankruptcy.`,facts:[`Coleco's Telstar console of 1976 sold about a million units.`,`Coleco filed for bankruptcy in July 1988.`]},"company:nintendont":{intro:`Modelled on Nintendo, founded in Kyoto by Fusajiro Yamauchi to make hanafuda, Japanese playing cards printed with flowers for the months of the year. The cards were made by hand from the bark of mulberry trees, and they sold to card players and to the gambling houses of the city.`,how:`Nintendo printed and painted its cards by hand and sold them in Kyoto and Osaka, where gamblers bought fresh decks often, which kept demand steady. Its name is often read as leave luck to heaven, and its business was games of chance and skill, sold through shops across the region.`,history:`Fusajiro Yamauchi founded the company in Kyoto in September 1889. In 1959 it began printing Disney characters on Western-style playing cards, which sold well to families. In 1980 Gunpei Yokoi's Game & Watch handhelds put a game and a clock in a pocket. In 1983 it launched the Famicom in Japan, which reached America in 1985 as the Nintendo Entertainment System and revived video games there after the crash. Its Game Boy of 1989 and Wii of 2006 each sold more than 100 million units. In 2017 it launched the Switch, a home console that could be carried as a handheld, and in 2025 the Switch 2 followed.`,facts:[`The Game Boy family sold about 119 million units from 1989.`,`The Wii of 2006 sold about 102 million units.`,`The Switch had sold more than 150 million units by 2025.`]},"company:segue":{intro:`Modelled on Sega, which grew from Service Games, a business set up by Americans to supply jukeboxes and slot machines to American military bases in Japan. Its name was short for Service Games, and it became a supplier and operator of coin-operated amusement machines in Japan.`,how:`Sega imported, built and ran coin-operated machines, from jukeboxes and slot machines to photo booths and electro-mechanical games. It earned money from the coins dropped into the machines it placed and from selling machines to other operators, and it began designing amusement games of its own.`,history:`In 1960 Service Games' Japanese business was reorganised as Nihon Goraku Bussan, and in 1965 it merged with David Rosen's company to form Sega Enterprises. In 1966 its submarine game Periscope was a hit in arcades around the world. In 1988 it launched the Mega Drive, sold in America from 1989 as the Genesis, and in 1991 Sonic the Hedgehog became its star. The Saturn of 1994 sold poorly, and the Dreamcast of 1998, with a built-in modem, could not hold off Sony's PlayStation 2. In 2001 Sega stopped making consoles and became a publisher of games for other companies' machines. In 2004 it merged with Sammy, a maker of pachinko machines.`,facts:[`The Mega Drive and Genesis sold about 30 million consoles from 1988.`,`The Dreamcast sold about 9 million units before Sega stopped making it in 2001.`]},"company:phony":{intro:`Modelled on Sony Computer Entertainment, the video game company set up by the Japanese electronics giant Sony with its music arm. It was born from a broken partnership: Nintendo had asked Sony to build a CD-ROM add-on for its console and then dropped the deal, and the engineer Ken Kutaragi persuaded Sony to make a console of its own.`,how:`Sony's console, the PlayStation, read games from cheap CDs instead of costly cartridges and had chips for drawing 3D graphics. Sony sold the machine at a low price and made its money from licence fees on every game sold by outside publishers, whom it courted with easy tools and low costs.`,history:`Sony Computer Entertainment was founded in November 1993, and the PlayStation launched in Japan in December 1994 and in America and Europe in 1995. By 2004 the PlayStation had passed 100 million sales, the first console to do so. The PlayStation 2 of 2000 became the best-selling console in history. In 2006 the costly PlayStation 3 struggled at first against Microsoft's Xbox 360 and Nintendo's Wii. The PlayStation 4 of 2013 sold more than 115 million units. In 2016 the company became Sony Interactive Entertainment, and in 2020 it launched the PlayStation 5.`,facts:[`The PlayStation 2 of 2000 sold about 160 million units, a record.`,`The PlayStation launched in Japan on 3 December 1994.`]},"company:taitoe":{intro:`Modelled on Taito, a company founded in Tokyo by Michael Kogan, a Russian-born Jewish businessman who had grown up in Manchuria. It began by importing and distilling vodka and then moved into vending machines and jukeboxes, placing coin-operated machines in shops and bars across Japan.`,how:`Taito imported, made and ran coin-operated machines, from jukeboxes and vending machines to simple amusement games, earning money both from selling machines and from the coins dropped into those it operated. Its network of locations around Japan gave it a ready market for any new coin machine it built.`,history:`Michael Kogan founded the Taito Trading Company in Tokyo in 1953. In 1973 it began making video arcade games. In 1978 its Space Invaders, created by Tomohiro Nishikado, became a national craze in Japan, and some arcades filled up with nothing but Invaders machines. In 1986 it released two more hits, Bubble Bobble and Arkanoid. In 2005 the game publisher Square Enix bought Taito.`,facts:[`Taito sold more than 100,000 Space Invaders machines in Japan in 1978.`,`Square Enix bought Taito in 2005.`]},"company:nomco":{intro:`Modelled on Namco, founded by Masaya Nakamura with two mechanical horse rides on the roof of a department store in Yokohama. It grew by running children's rides and amusements in department stores, at a time when Japanese families went up to the rooftops of big shops for an outing.`,how:`Nakamura Manufacturing built and ran coin-operated rides and amusement machines on department store rooftops, earning money from the coins that children and parents spent. Its good relations with the big store chains gave it the best places for each new machine, and it moved into electro-mechanical arcade games.`,history:`Masaya Nakamura founded Nakamura Manufacturing in 1955 with two rides on the roof of a Yokohama department store. In 1974 it bought the struggling Japanese arm of Atari. In 1977 it adopted the name Namco, and in 1979 it released Galaxian, a space shooting game in full colour. In 1980 Toru Iwatani's Pac-Man became one of the most popular arcade games ever made. In 1993 Ridge Racer and in 1994 Tekken were hits in arcades and on Sony's PlayStation. In 2005 Namco merged with the toy maker Bandai to form Bandai Namco.`,facts:[`Namco bought Atari's Japanese arm in 1974.`,`Nearly 300,000 Pac-Man arcade cabinets were sold between 1981 and 1987.`]},"company:midwhy":{intro:`Modelled on Midway Manufacturing, a maker of coin-operated amusement machines from the Chicago area, the heart of the American pinball and arcade industry. It built electro-mechanical games, such as shooting galleries and baseball machines, for bars and arcades, and here it stands for a family of Chicago firms including Bally and Williams.`,how:`Midway designed and built arcade machines and sold them through distributors to operators, who placed them in bars, bowling alleys and arcades and shared the coins with the owners of each spot. It also licensed games from other makers, building and selling them for the American market.`,history:`Midway was founded in 1958 in Schiller Park, Illinois, and in 1969 the pinball maker Bally bought it. In 1975 its Gun Fight, licensed from Taito, was one of the first arcade games to use a microprocessor. In 1978 it brought Space Invaders to America and in 1980 Pac-Man, both huge hits. Williams, a Chicago rival, made Defender in 1981, bought Bally's game business in 1988 and released Mortal Kombat under the Midway name in 1992. Midway turned to home console games in the 1990s but lost money, and in February 2009 it filed for bankruptcy.`,facts:[`Gun Fight of 1975 ran on an Intel 8080 microprocessor.`,`Warner Bros bought most of Midway, with Mortal Kombat, for about $33 million in 2009.`]},"company:kapowcom":{intro:`Modelled on Capcom, a Japanese company from Osaka founded by Kenzo Tsujimoto as a maker of electronic game machines. Its name was a short form of Capsule Computers, its phrase for game machines packed full of play, and it set out to make video games for the arcades.`,how:`Capcom designed games for arcade machines and sold the cabinets and circuit boards to operators, and it adapted its hits for home consoles, earning money twice from each game. It built a reputation for tight controls, bold characters and colourful, well-drawn graphics.`,history:`Kenzo Tsujimoto founded the company in 1979, and it took the name Capcom in 1983. In 1987 it released Mega Man on Nintendo's console and the first Street Fighter in arcades. In 1991 Street Fighter II started a worldwide boom in head-to-head fighting games. In 1996 Resident Evil defined survival horror on Sony's PlayStation. In 2018 Monster Hunter: World became its best-selling game, with more than 20 million copies sold by 2024.`,facts:[`Street Fighter II sold about 6 million copies on the Super Nintendo from 1992.`,`Monster Hunter: World of 2018 passed 20 million copies by 2024.`]},"company:hyperactivision":{intro:`Modelled on Activision, founded by four programmers who left Atari, David Crane, Larry Kaplan, Alan Miller and Bob Whitehead, with the record industry executive Jim Levy. They were angry that Atari paid them a salary but gave them no credit or royalties for hit games, and they set out to make games for Atari's console on their own.`,how:`Activision made game cartridges for the Atari Video Computer System and sold them in shops alongside Atari's own, without Atari's blessing. It printed its designers' names on the boxes and treated them like recording artists, and it kept the whole profit on each cartridge it sold.`,history:`The founders set up Activision in October 1979, the first independent company to make games for another firm's console. Atari sued to stop it, but in 1982 the two settled, and games from outside publishers became normal. In 1982 David Crane's Pitfall! sold millions of copies. The 1983 crash nearly sank the company, and in 1991 Bobby Kotick took it over and brought it through bankruptcy. In 2003 it published Call of Duty, which grew into one of the best-selling game series. In 2008 it merged with Vivendi's games arm to form Activision Blizzard, and in October 2023 Microsoft bought it for about $69 billion.`,facts:[`Pitfall! of 1982 sold about 4 million copies.`,`Microsoft paid about $69 billion for Activision Blizzard in 2023.`]},"company:hearts":{intro:`Modelled on Electronic Arts, a game publisher founded in San Mateo, California, by Trip Hawkins, a former marketing manager at Apple. It treated game designers as software artists, printing their names and photos on boxes that looked like record album covers, and it set out to publish games for the home computers of the day.`,how:`EA paid outside development teams to make games and handled the packaging, marketing and distribution itself, selling straight to shops instead of through middlemen. It licensed famous names, such as sports stars, to help sell its games, and it spread its risk by publishing many titles from many teams.`,history:`Trip Hawkins founded Electronic Arts in 1982, and in 1983 a famous advert asked whether a computer could make you cry. In 1988 it released John Madden Football, which grew into its yearly Madden NFL series. In 1993 it released FIFA International Soccer, the first of its FIFA football games. In 2000 it published The Sims, made by Will Wright's Maxis studio, which EA had bought in 1997. In 2023 its football games lost the FIFA name and became EA Sports FC. In August 2026 a group led by Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund completed a $55 billion purchase of EA, taking it private.`,facts:[`The buyout completed in 2026 valued EA at about $55 billion.`,`EA's football series dropped the FIFA name in 2023 after 30 years.`]},"company:infogrue":{intro:`Modelled on Infocom, a company formed in Cambridge, Massachusetts, by staff and students of MIT's Dynamic Modelling Group who had written Zork, a text adventure game, on the laboratory's big PDP-10 computer. Players typed commands in plain English, such as open the mailbox, and the game described in prose what happened.`,how:`Infocom wrote its games in a special language that ran on a virtual machine, the Z-machine, so each new game needed only a small interpreter for each home computer, and the same game could be sold for many machines at once. Its boxes came with feelies, small objects and documents that added to the story and helped stop copying.`,history:`Infocom was founded in June 1979 by MIT staff and students including Marc Blank and Dave Lebling. In 1980 it released Zork I, first for the TRS-80 and then for other home computers. In 1984 it published The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy, written with Douglas Adams. It lost heavily on Cornerstone, a business database program, in 1985, and in 1986 Activision bought it. In 1989 Activision closed Infocom's offices in Cambridge.`,facts:[`Activision bought Infocom for about $7.5 million in 1986.`,`Infocom published more than 30 text adventures between 1980 and 1989.`]},"company:sierraoff":{intro:`Modelled on Sierra On-Line, a game company started by Ken and Roberta Williams in Los Angeles and soon moved to Oakhurst, in the foothills of the Sierra Nevada near Yosemite. Roberta, a fan of text adventures, wanted an adventure with pictures, and Ken, a programmer, wrote it for the Apple II.`,how:`Sierra's adventure games showed scenes in colour graphics and let players type commands to solve puzzles and move through a story. It sold games on floppy disks through computer shops and by mail, and it built its own game engines so that designers could turn out new adventures quickly for many different computers.`,history:`Ken and Roberta Williams founded On-Line Systems in 1979, and in 1980 their Mystery House was the first home computer adventure game with pictures. In 1982 the company renamed itself Sierra On-Line. In 1984 King's Quest, made for IBM's PCjr computer, let players walk a character around animated scenes. Its Space Quest, Police Quest and Leisure Suit Larry series followed in the late 1980s. In 1996 the company CUC International bought Sierra for about $1 billion. In 1998 Sierra published Half-Life, the first game by the young studio Valve. In 2008 the Sierra name passed to Activision Blizzard.`,facts:[`Mystery House of 1980 was the first graphical adventure for a home computer.`,`CUC International bought Sierra for about $1 billion in 1996.`]},"company:origami":{intro:`Modelled on Origin Systems, a game company founded by Richard Garriott, who called himself Lord British, with his brother Robert, their father Owen, a NASA astronaut, and others. It made role-playing games for home computers, led by Garriott's Ultima series, in which players explored a fantasy world, fought monsters and talked to its people.`,how:`Origin's games were big, detailed worlds that players explored for weeks, sold in boxes packed with cloth maps, coins and other trinkets. It earned money from each boxed game, and its motto, We create worlds, summed up its taste for depth and story over quick action.`,history:`Richard and Robert Garriott founded Origin Systems in 1983. In 1985 Ultima IV asked players to live by eight virtues rather than simply kill monsters. In 1990 Chris Roberts's Wing Commander, a space combat game with film-like scenes, became a big hit. In 1992 Electronic Arts bought Origin. In 1997 it launched Ultima Online, one of the first big online role-playing worlds, which drew more than 100,000 paying players within a year. In 2004 EA closed Origin. In 2008 Richard Garriott flew to the International Space Station as a paying space tourist.`,facts:[`Ultima Online passed 100,000 subscribers within about a year of its launch in 1997.`,`Richard Garriott paid his own way to the International Space Station in 2008.`]},"company:macroprose":{intro:`Modelled on MicroProse, a game company from Hunt Valley, Maryland, founded by Bill Stealey, a former US Air Force pilot, and Sid Meier, a programmer he met at work. Stealey wanted flight simulators that felt like the real thing, and Meier could write them.`,how:`MicroProse made flight and military simulations for home computers such as the Atari 800 and Commodore 64, sold in boxes with thick manuals full of real tactics. Stealey marketed them with an aviator's swagger, while Meier looked for the fun in each subject, from submarines to pirates.`,history:`Bill Stealey and Sid Meier founded MicroProse in 1982. In 1984 F-15 Strike Eagle became one of its biggest sellers. In 1987 Pirates! was the first game sold with Sid Meier's name in its title. In 1991 Meier's Civilization let players guide a people from the Stone Age to the space age, one turn at a time. In 1993 Spectrum HoloByte bought MicroProse, and in 1994 it published X-COM: UFO Defense. In 1998 the toy company Hasbro bought MicroProse, and by 2003 its old studios had closed.`,facts:[`Pirates! of 1987 was the first game with Sid Meier's name in its title.`,`Hasbro bought MicroProse for about $70 million in 1998.`]},"company:perigee":{intro:`Modelled on Apogee Software, a game publisher from Garland, Texas, near Dallas, founded by Scott Miller. Its twist was shareware: it gave away the first part of each game free, through bulletin boards and disk catalogues, and sold the rest by post to players who wanted more.`,how:`Under Apogee's model, the first episode of a game could be copied and shared freely; players who enjoyed it rang or wrote to order the remaining episodes. This cost little in advertising and let small teams reach huge numbers of PC owners, with the free episode doing the selling.`,history:`Scott Miller published Kingdom of Kroz in 1987, giving away its first episode, the start of the Apogee model. In 1990 Apogee published Commander Keen, made by the young programmers who founded id Software in 1991. In 1991 it released Duke Nukem, and in 1992 it published id's Wolfenstein 3D. In 1994 it began using the name 3D Realms for its 3D games, and in 1996 Duke Nukem 3D was a huge hit. Its sequel, Duke Nukem Forever, was announced in 1997 and only came out in 2011, finished by another company.`,facts:[`Kingdom of Kroz of 1987 was Apogee's first shareware game.`,`Duke Nukem Forever took 14 years, from its announcement in 1997 to release in 2011.`]},"company:egosoftware":{intro:`Modelled on id Software, a small game studio from Texas founded by John Carmack, John Romero, Tom Hall and Adrian Carmack, who had worked together at the software firm Softdisk in Shreveport, Louisiana. John Carmack's clever programming made smooth scrolling and fast 3D graphics possible on ordinary home PCs.`,how:`id made action games for PCs and sold them as shareware: the first episode was free to copy, and players ordered the rest. Carmack's game engines drew fast first-person views, and id licensed them to other studios, while it let players build their own levels and changes to its games.`,history:`John Carmack, John Romero, Tom Hall and Adrian Carmack founded id in February 1991, after their Commander Keen game, published by Apogee in 1990, became a hit. In 1992 Wolfenstein 3D made the first-person shooter popular. In December 1993 Doom, with network play and levels that players could change, was released as shareware and spread to millions of PCs. In 1996 Quake brought true 3D worlds and online play. In 2009 ZeniMax Media, the owner of Bethesda, bought id. In 2021 Microsoft bought ZeniMax.`,facts:[`Doom was released as shareware on 10 December 1993.`,`Microsoft paid $7.5 billion for ZeniMax, id's owner, in 2021.`]},"company:drizzle":{intro:`Modelled on Blizzard Entertainment, a game studio from Irvine, California, founded as Silicon & Synapse by three graduates of the University of California, Los Angeles: Allen Adham, Michael Morhaime and Frank Pearce. It began by converting other companies' games for new machines and making console games of its own.`,how:`Silicon & Synapse earned money by porting games to new computers and consoles for other publishers, and used it to fund its own games. Its founders' rule was to keep polishing a game until it was really fun, even if that meant delays, a habit that became the studio's trademark.`,history:`Allen Adham, Michael Morhaime and Frank Pearce founded Silicon & Synapse in 1991, and in 1994 it renamed itself Blizzard Entertainment. In 1994 its Warcraft: Orcs & Humans helped make real-time strategy popular. In 1996 Diablo launched with Battle.net, a free online service for finding other players, and in 1998 StarCraft followed, which became a spectator sport in South Korea. In 2004 World of Warcraft launched, and in 2010 it had about 12 million paying subscribers. In 2008 Blizzard became part of Activision Blizzard, which Microsoft bought in 2023.`,facts:[`World of Warcraft had about 12 million paying subscribers in 2010.`,`Battle.net, launched with Diablo in 1996, let players meet online for free.`]},"company:faucet":{intro:`Modelled on Valve, a game company from near Seattle founded by Gabe Newell and Mike Harrington, two Microsoft employees who had grown rich at the software giant. They set out to make a first-person action game that told its story through play, without stopping for long cut scenes.`,how:`Valve licensed id Software's Quake engine and reworked it heavily for its first game, and it hired people from the player community who had made their own levels and modifications. It ran with an unusually flat structure, in which staff chose which projects to work on.`,history:`Gabe Newell and Mike Harrington founded Valve in Kirkland, Washington, in August 1996. In November 1998 its first game, Half-Life, published by Sierra, won wide praise. In 2000 Valve hired the makers of Counter-Strike, a fan-made modification of Half-Life, and turned it into a best-selling game. In 2003 it launched Steam, a program for updating games and selling them online. In 2004 Half-Life 2 required Steam, and by 2011 Steam was the largest online store for PC games. In 2022 Valve released the Steam Deck, a handheld PC.`,facts:[`Valve hired Counter-Strike's makers and bought the game in 2000.`,`Steam had nearly 40 million users online at the same time in 2025.`]},"company:epick":{intro:`Modelled on Epic Games, which began as Potomac Computer Systems, a one-man company that Tim Sweeney ran from his parents' house in Potomac, Maryland. His first game, ZZT, an adventure drawn in text characters, came with its own simple editor so that players could make their own worlds, and it was sold as shareware.`,how:`Sweeney sold shareware games by post and began publishing games by other small developers, sharing the income with them. His strength was technology: tools and engines that made games easier to build and change, which could be sold to other companies as well as used in his own games.`,history:`Tim Sweeney released ZZT in 1991, and in 1992 the company became Epic MegaGames. In 1998 its shooter Unreal introduced the Unreal Engine, which Epic licensed to many other studios. In 1999 it moved to Cary, North Carolina, and took the name Epic Games. In 2006 Gears of War for the Xbox 360 was a big hit. In 2012 the Chinese company Tencent bought about 40 per cent of Epic. In 2017 Fortnite's free Battle Royale mode drew millions of players, and in 2018 Epic opened its own online game store.`,facts:[`Tencent paid about $330 million for about 40 per cent of Epic in 2012.`,`Fortnite had about 125 million players by June 2018, within a year of launch.`]},"company:taketoo":{intro:`Modelled on Take-Two Interactive, a video game publisher founded in New York by Ryan Brant, together with its most famous label, Rockstar Games. It started as a small developer of games for computers and consoles in a Manhattan office, and set out to grow by buying other studios and publishers.`,how:`Take-Two funded, marketed and sold games made by its own studios and by outside developers, earning money from each game sold in shops. Buying smaller publishers and studios brought it their games, their teams and their places on shop shelves, and it gave its best studios freedom to take years over a game.`,history:`Ryan Brant founded Take-Two Interactive in New York in September 1993. In 1998 it bought BMG Interactive, the publisher of the first Grand Theft Auto, and turned it into Rockstar Games, led by the brothers Sam and Dan Houser. In 2001 Grand Theft Auto III, set in a 3D city that players could roam freely, was a huge hit. In 2013 Grand Theft Auto V took about $1 billion in its first three days on sale. In 2022 Take-Two bought Zynga, the mobile games company, for about $12.7 billion. By 2025 Grand Theft Auto V had sold more than 200 million copies.`,facts:[`Grand Theft Auto V took about $1 billion in its first three days in 2013.`,`Take-Two paid about $12.7 billion for Zynga in 2022.`,`Grand Theft Auto V passed 200 million copies sold by 2025.`]},"company:youbisoft":{intro:`Modelled on Ubisoft, a French video game company founded by the five Guillemot brothers from Carentoir in Brittany, whose family business sold farm supplies to the region's farmers. The brothers began by importing and distributing other publishers' games in France, and then hired developers to make games of their own.`,how:`Ubisoft first earned money by distributing games to French shops, and it then built its own studios, in France and abroad, with large teams making big games in long-running series. The Guillemot family kept control of the company while selling shares on the stock market.`,history:`The Guillemot brothers founded Ubisoft in March 1986. In 1995 Rayman, created by Michel Ancel, was its first big hit. In 1997 it opened a studio in Montreal, which by the 2010s was one of the largest game studios in the world. In 2004 Electronic Arts bought a stake of about a fifth, which Ubisoft treated as a hostile move. In 2007 Assassin's Creed began its biggest series. From 2015 to 2018 it fought off a takeover attempt by the French media group Vivendi. In 2025 Tencent agreed to invest about €1.16 billion in a new Ubisoft company holding Assassin's Creed and other series.`,facts:[`Vivendi sold its stake of about 27 per cent back in 2018.`,`Tencent agreed in 2025 to invest about €1.16 billion in a Ubisoft subsidiary.`]},"company:mojank":{intro:`Modelled on Mojang, a Swedish game studio from Stockholm founded by Markus Persson, known as Notch, as his game Minecraft grew too big for a hobby. Minecraft was a world built of blocks that players could dig, mine and build with, alone or together, with no set goal.`,how:`Mojang sold Minecraft directly to players over the internet while it was still unfinished, at a low price that rose as features were added, so that early fans paid for its development. Players filmed their creations and shared them online, which spread word of the game for free.`,history:`Markus Persson released the first version of Minecraft in May 2009. In 2010 he turned Mojang into a full company, with Jakob Porsér and Carl Manneh, to develop it. Minecraft was declared finished in November 2011, at the MineCon gathering in Las Vegas. In 2014 Microsoft bought Mojang for $2.5 billion. By 2023 Minecraft had sold more than 300 million copies, the best-selling video game ever. In 2025 A Minecraft Movie earned more than $900 million at cinemas.`,facts:[`Microsoft paid $2.5 billion for Mojang in 2014.`,`Minecraft passed 300 million copies sold in 2023.`]},"company:nexxon":{intro:`Modelled on Nexon, a South Korean game company founded in Seoul by Kim Jung-ju with a fellow student, Song Jae-kyung, to make online games. They set out to build graphical online worlds where players could meet and adventure together over the internet, at a time when very few people had tried it.`,how:`Nexon ran its games on its own servers and was among the first to make them free to play, earning money by selling virtual items, such as clothes for players' characters, through small payments. Korea's many internet cafes, called PC bangs, gave it a big audience of young players.`,history:`Kim Jung-ju founded Nexon in Seoul in December 1994, and in 1996 it launched The Kingdom of the Winds, one of the first graphical online role-playing games. In 1999 its Quiz Quiz was one of the first games to be free to play and earn its money by selling items. In 2003 MapleStory, a colourful side-scrolling online world, and in 2004 KartRider, a racing game, became huge hits in Korea. In 2005 Nexon moved its headquarters to Tokyo, and in December 2011 it listed on the Tokyo Stock Exchange. Kim Jung-ju died in 2022.`,facts:[`Nexon raised about $1.2 billion in its Tokyo share sale of 2011.`,`The Kingdom of the Winds of 1996 was one of the first graphical online role-playing games.`]},"company:ncsofa":{intro:`Modelled on NCsoft, a South Korean game company founded in Seoul by Kim Taek-jin, a programmer who had helped write one of Korea's first Korean-language word processors. It set out to make online role-playing games, in which thousands of players share one fantasy world over the internet.`,how:`NCsoft charged players a monthly fee to enter its worlds, and many played from Korea's internet cafes, which bought access in bulk. Its games ran on its own servers, where players fought, traded and formed clans called blood pledges that battled each other for castles.`,history:`Kim Taek-jin founded NCsoft in March 1997, and in September 1998 it launched Lineage, based on a Korean comic. In 2001 it bought Destination Games, Richard Garriott's studio, to enter America. In 2003 Lineage II followed, and in 2008 Aion. In 2012 it launched Guild Wars 2, made by its American studio ArenaNet. In 2015 the rival Nexon, which had bought a big stake in 2012, clashed with Kim over control and then sold its shares.`,facts:[`Lineage launched in Korea in September 1998.`,`Guild Wars 2 sold more than 2 million copies within weeks of its launch in 2012.`]},"company:tencents":{intro:`Modelled on Tencent, an internet company founded in Shenzhen, China, by Ma Huateng, known as Pony Ma, with four classmates and colleagues. Its first product was a free instant messaging program for Chinese computer users, based on the idea of the ICQ program.`,how:`Tencent gave its messenger away free and made money by selling small extras to its millions of users, such as cartoon avatars, ringtones and virtual currency. It built services and games around its messaging, so that friends who chatted could also play together, and it invested in many game companies outside China.`,history:`Pony Ma and four co-founders set up Tencent in November 1998, and in 1999 it launched OICQ, renamed QQ in 2000 after a legal complaint from ICQ's owner, AOL. It listed on the Hong Kong stock exchange in 2004. In 2011 it launched WeChat, a mobile messaging app that grew into a platform for payments and services. In 2011 it took control of Riot Games, in 2012 it bought about 40 per cent of Epic Games, and in 2016 it bought most of Supercell. In 2015 its Honor of Kings came out, and by 2023 Tencent was the biggest games company in the world by revenue.`,facts:[`Tencent's group paid about $8.6 billion for most of Supercell in 2016.`,`WeChat had more than 1.3 billion monthly users in 2023.`]},"company:neteasy":{intro:`Modelled on NetEase, a Chinese internet company founded by William Ding, a young engineer from Ningbo who had worked at the city's telecom office. It began with a Chinese-language search service and free e-mail, a portal for the first wave of internet users in China.`,how:`NetEase ran free websites and e-mail, paid for by advertising, and sold extra services such as text messages for mobile phones. Its engineers built their own software instead of licensing foreign products, and it watched closely what China's young internet users wanted.`,history:`William Ding founded NetEase in Guangzhou in June 1997. It listed on the Nasdaq stock market in 2000, and trading in its shares was halted in 2001 over an accounting error. In 2001 it launched its first big online game, Westward Journey, and in 2003 Fantasy Westward Journey, which became one of China's most played games. In 2009 it began running Blizzard's World of Warcraft in China, a partnership that ended in January 2023. In 2021 its battle game Naraka: Bladepoint became a hit around the world.`,facts:[`NetEase listed its shares on Nasdaq in 2000.`,`Blizzard's games went offline in China in January 2023 when the NetEase deal ended.`]},"company:rowdy":{intro:`Modelled on Riot Games, a Los Angeles game studio founded by Brandon Beck and Marc Merrill, two university friends who loved Defense of the Ancients, a fan-made battle map for Warcraft III. They wanted to make one free online game that people would keep playing for years, instead of a new boxed game every year.`,how:`Riot's game was free to download and play; it made money by selling champions, the characters players control, and skins, cosmetic outfits for them, without selling advantages in battle. It ran the game as a service with constant updates, and it built professional leagues to keep players watching.`,history:`Brandon Beck and Marc Merrill founded Riot in 2006, and League of Legends launched in October 2009. In 2011 Tencent bought a majority of Riot, and it took full ownership in 2015. In 2013 its world championship final filled the Staples Center in Los Angeles. In 2014 Riot said about 67 million people played League of Legends each month. In 2020 it launched Valorant, a team shooting game. In 2021 it agreed to pay $100 million to settle a lawsuit over discrimination against women.`,facts:[`League of Legends had about 67 million players a month in 2014.`,`Riot agreed to pay $100 million to settle a discrimination case in 2021.`]},"company:zinger":{intro:`Modelled on Zynga, a San Francisco company founded by Mark Pincus to make social games, simple games played inside social networks such as Facebook. It was named after Pincus's American bulldog, Zinga, and its games spread from friend to friend as players invited others to help them.`,how:`Zynga's games were free and earned money from small payments for virtual goods, such as seeds, tractors or energy to keep playing, and from adverts. They used players' friends lists to grow: requests and gifts posted to friends' news feeds brought in new players, and the company measured every click to tune its games.`,history:`Mark Pincus founded Zynga in 2007, and its first hit was a poker game on Facebook. In June 2009 it launched FarmVille, which drew more than 80 million players a month at its peak in 2010. It went public in December 2011, valued at about $7 billion. In 2012 it bought OMGPOP, the maker of Draw Something, for about $180 million, but players soon faded, and as Facebook changed its rules Zynga's shares lost about three quarters of their value that year. In 2022 Take-Two bought Zynga for about $12.7 billion.`,facts:[`FarmVille had more than 80 million monthly players at its peak in 2010.`,`Take-Two paid about $12.7 billion for Zynga in 2022.`]},"company:ravioli":{intro:`Modelled on Rovio Entertainment, a Finnish game company from Espoo, founded as Relude by three students from the Helsinki University of Technology after they won a mobile game contest. It made games for mobile phones, often for other publishers, in the years when phones had small screens and buttons.`,how:`Rovio built games to order for phone makers and publishers while trying to create a hit of its own. It earned money from development fees and from sales through phone operators' download stores, and it made dozens of games in the search for one that would take off.`,history:`Niklas Hed, Jarno Väkeväinen and Kim Dikert founded the company in 2003 after winning a mobile game contest, and it took the name Rovio in 2005. In December 2009 it released Angry Birds for Apple's iPhone, a game of flinging birds at pigs' fortresses, which was its 52nd game. In 2012 Angry Birds games passed a billion downloads, and Rovio sold toys and licensed theme parks. In 2016 The Angry Birds Movie earned about $350 million at cinemas. In 2023 Sega bought Rovio for about $776 million.`,facts:[`Angry Birds, released in 2009, was Rovio's 52nd game.`,`Angry Birds games passed a billion downloads in 2012.`,`Sega paid about $776 million for Rovio in 2023.`]},"company:cling":{intro:`Modelled on King, a game company founded in Stockholm and London as Midasplayer by Riccardo Zacconi, Sebastian Knutsson, Lars Markgren and others. It made quick skill games, such as puzzles and word games, for its own website, where players could compete in tournaments for prizes.`,how:`King's games were short, casual and played in a web browser, and players paid to enter tournaments or to play without adverts. It also supplied its games to big web portals, sharing the income, and it learned which games kept players coming back.`,history:`The founders set up the company, at first called Midasplayer, in 2003. In 2011 it moved onto Facebook, and in 2012 Candy Crush Saga, a game of swapping coloured sweets to make rows of three, came out on Facebook and then on phones. In 2013 Candy Crush was among the most played games in the world, and King's revenue reached about $1.9 billion. King went public in New York in March 2014, and its shares fell about 16 per cent on the first day. In 2016 Activision Blizzard bought King for $5.9 billion.`,facts:[`King's revenue was about $1.9 billion in 2013.`,`Activision Blizzard paid $5.9 billion for King in 2016.`]},"company:supersell":{intro:`Modelled on Supercell, a Finnish game company from Helsinki founded by Ilkka Paananen, Mikko Kodisoja and four others who had worked on games for the web and phones. It organised itself in small, independent teams called cells, each free to make, test and cancel its own game ideas.`,how:`Supercell made free games for tablets and smartphones that earned money from small payments, such as gems to speed up building a village. A cell of five to seven people would test a game in a few countries and cancel it if players did not stay, so that only a few games were released, each meant to last for years.`,history:`Ilkka Paananen and five co-founders set up Supercell in Helsinki in 2010, at first making a game for web browsers. In 2012 it released Hay Day and Clash of Clans for the iPad and iPhone, and both were huge hits. In 2013 the Japanese companies SoftBank and GungHo bought just over half of Supercell for about $1.5 billion. In 2016 it released Clash Royale, and that year a group led by Tencent bought 84 per cent of the company for about $8.6 billion. In 2018 Brawl Stars followed.`,facts:[`SoftBank and GungHo paid about $1.5 billion for 51 per cent of Supercell in 2013.`,`A group led by Tencent paid about $8.6 billion for 84 per cent of Supercell in 2016.`]},"company:antic":{intro:`Modelled on Niantic, which began as Niantic Labs, a start-up inside Google led by John Hanke, who had co-founded Keyhole, the company behind Google Earth. Named after a Gold Rush ship whose hull lies buried under San Francisco, it set out to make apps and games that got people outside, using the maps and location in smartphones.`,how:`Niantic's games put virtual objects on a map of the real world, so that players had to walk to real places, such as statues, murals and churches, to play. They were free and made money from small payments for items and from sponsored locations, such as shops and restaurants that paid to become places in the game.`,history:`John Hanke set up Niantic Labs inside Google in 2010. In 2012 it released Ingress, a game in which two teams battled to control real-world landmarks. In 2015 Niantic became an independent company, with investment from Google, Nintendo and The Pokémon Company. In July 2016 Pokémon GO became a worldwide craze, drawing crowds of players into parks and streets. In 2025 Niantic sold its games business, including Pokémon GO, to Scopely for $3.5 billion and spun off its mapping technology as Niantic Spatial.`,facts:[`Pokémon GO passed 500 million downloads within about two months of its launch in 2016.`,`Scopely paid $3.5 billion for Niantic's games in 2025.`]},"company:roblocks":{intro:`Modelled on Roblox, a company founded by David Baszucki and Erik Cassel, who had earlier made Interactive Physics, a program that let schoolchildren build and test experiments on a computer. Their new idea was an online world where young people could build their own games with blocks and simple code, and share them with others.`,how:`Roblox was free to play, and the games in it were made by its users with its Roblox Studio tools and a programming language called Lua. Players bought a virtual currency, Robux, to spend on avatar items and extras inside games, and Roblox shared part of that money with the creators of popular games.`,history:`David Baszucki and Erik Cassel founded the company in 2004 under the name DynaBlocks, and it became Roblox in 2005. The Roblox platform opened to the public in 2006. In 2013 it began letting creators exchange the Robux they earned for real money, and that year Erik Cassel died of cancer. During the lockdowns of 2020 its player numbers surged. In March 2021 it listed on the New York Stock Exchange, valued at about $38 billion on its first day. By the end of 2024 it had more than 80 million daily users.`,facts:[`Roblox was valued at about $38 billion on its first day of trading in 2021.`,`Roblox had more than 80 million daily users at the end of 2024.`]},"company:disunity":{intro:`Modelled on Unity Technologies, a game engine company founded in Copenhagen by David Helgason, Joachim Ante and Nicholas Francis, who had set out to make a game of their own and found the tools more interesting. Their engine let small teams build a game once and run it on many kinds of computers and devices.`,how:`Unity sold its engine and editor to developers, with a cheap or free basic version and paid professional licences. Developers built scenes in its visual editor and wrote game code in common programming languages, while the engine handled graphics, physics and sound on each platform, so a small studio could reach players on many machines.`,history:`David Helgason, Joachim Ante and Nicholas Francis founded the company in Copenhagen in 2004, and Unity 1.0 was launched for the Mac at Apple's developer conference in June 2005. In 2008 Unity added support for Apple's iPhone, and by 2016 it powered about a third of the top free mobile games. It moved its headquarters to San Francisco and went public in September 2020. In 2022 it merged with an advertising technology company in a deal worth about $4.4 billion. In September 2023 it announced a fee for every install of a game made with Unity, and after a revolt by developers it scrapped the fee in 2024.`,facts:[`Unity 1.0 was launched for the Mac in 2005.`,`The merger with an advertising technology company in 2022 was worth about $4.4 billion.`]},"company:zerox":{intro:`Modelled on Xerox, which began as the Haloid Company of Rochester, New York, a small maker of photographic paper in the shadow of Kodak, the city's photographic giant. It sold sensitised paper for photographs and for copying documents by photography, a modest but steady trade.`,how:`Haloid coated paper with chemicals that darken in light and sold it to photographers and to offices that copied documents photographically. Being tiny next to Kodak, it was always searching for new products, and its owners were willing to gamble on untried inventions that bigger firms ignored.`,history:`The Haloid Company was founded in Rochester in 1906. In 1938 Chester Carlson, a patent attorney, made the first electrophotographic copy in a back room in Queens, New York, and in 1947 Haloid licensed his process, which it called xerography. In 1959 the Xerox 914, the first automatic office copier to use plain paper, was a sensation, and in 1961 the company renamed itself Xerox. In 1970 it opened the Palo Alto Research Center, where in the 1970s researchers invented the laser printer, Ethernet and the Alto computer with its windows and mouse. In 1980 Xerox bought Kurzweil Computer Products, the maker of reading machines. In 2016 it split off its business services arm as Conduent.`,facts:[`The Xerox 914 of 1959 made about seven copies a minute.`,`Researchers at Xerox PARC built the Alto computer in 1973.`]},"company:ncr":{intro:`Modelled on NCR, the National Cash Register Company of Dayton, Ohio, which John Henry Patterson, a former coal dealer, bought to make the new cash register. The machine had been invented by a Dayton saloon keeper to stop his staff pocketing the takings, and Patterson believed every shop in America needed one.`,how:`NCR's registers rang up each sale, showed the price to the customer and kept a locked running total, so a shopkeeper could tell whether money had gone missing. Patterson built the first modern sales force, with scripted sales talks, quotas, training schools and protected territories, and NCR grew by selling hard to every kind of shop.`,history:`John Henry Patterson bought the company in 1884 and named it the National Cash Register Company. In 1906 the engineer Charles Kettering designed an electric cash register for NCR. In 1913 Patterson and his sales manager, Thomas Watson, were convicted of crushing rivals illegally, a verdict overturned in 1915. In 1914 Patterson fired Watson, who went to run the company that he renamed IBM. In 1974 NCR changed its full name to NCR Corporation, as computers and bank machines took over from registers. In 1991 AT&T bought NCR for about $7.4 billion, and in 1996 it was spun off again. In 2023 NCR split in two, separating its cash machine business as NCR Atleos.`,facts:[`Charles Kettering's electric cash register appeared at NCR in 1906.`,`AT&T paid about $7.4 billion for NCR in 1991.`]},"company:cymbalics":{intro:`Modelled on Symbolics, a company founded by a group of programmers from the MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory to build and sell Lisp machines, computers designed to run Lisp, the favourite programming language of AI research. Each was a powerful single-user workstation with a large screen and a mouse, at a time when most programmers shared one big computer.`,how:`Symbolics machines ran Lisp directly in their hardware, with special support for the language's tagged data, and came with a rich programming environment in which everything, down to the operating system, was written in Lisp and could be changed while running. They cost tens of thousands of dollars each and sold to AI laboratories, defence contractors and companies building expert systems.`,history:`Russell Noftsker and colleagues from MIT founded Symbolics in 1980, and its first machine, the LM-2, shipped in 1981. In 1983 it launched the 3600 series, its own improved design. On 15 March 1985 it registered symbolics.com, the first commercial internet domain name. In 1987 the market for Lisp machines began to collapse, as cheaper Unix workstations from Sun and others ran Lisp well enough and the expert system boom faded. In 1993 Symbolics filed for bankruptcy protection. In 2009 the symbolics.com domain was sold to an investor.`,facts:[`symbolics.com, registered on 15 March 1985, was the first commercial internet domain.`,`The symbolics.com domain was sold to an investor in 2009.`]},"company:lithp":{intro:`Modelled on Lisp Machines Inc, or LMI, the company Richard Greenblatt set up to sell Lisp machines based on the design he had helped build at the MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory. Greenblatt, a legendary hacker who had written one of the first strong chess programs, wanted a small company that shared its work with the lab rather than a giant run by investors.`,how:`LMI built Lisp machines, workstations designed to run the Lisp programming language at full speed, under licence from MIT, and sold them to research laboratories and AI firms. It grew slowly, relying on sales rather than venture capital, and it kept close ties with the hackers at MIT.`,history:`Richard Greenblatt founded Lisp Machines Inc in 1979. In 1980 his former colleagues set up Symbolics, a rival backed by venture capital, which hired most of the lab's Lisp programmers. Angered by the split, Richard Stallman spent 1982 and 1983 copying Symbolics' improvements into MIT's own Lisp machine software, which LMI could use. In 1984 Stallman began the GNU project for free software, partly in reaction to that fight. In 1987 LMI went bankrupt as the market for Lisp machines collapsed.`,facts:[`Greenblatt's Mac Hack VI became the first chess program to play in human tournaments, in 1967.`,`LMI went bankrupt in 1987.`]},"company:teknowitall":{intro:`Modelled on Teknowledge, a company from Palo Alto, California, founded by Edward Feigenbaum and other artificial intelligence researchers from Stanford University to sell expert systems. An expert system was a program that captured the rules of thumb of a human expert, such as a doctor or an oil geologist, so that a computer could give advice in that field.`,how:`Teknowledge sold software tools, called shells, for building expert systems, along with training and consulting to help companies interview their experts and turn what they knew into rules. Its customers were big firms hoping to bottle the wisdom of their best staff and spread it across the company.`,history:`Edward Feigenbaum, Frederick Hayes-Roth and colleagues founded Teknowledge in 1981. Its founders drew on Stanford's work on expert systems such as MYCIN, a program of the 1970s that advised doctors on infections. In 1984 it released M.1, an expert system shell for the IBM PC, at the height of excitement about artificial intelligence. As the AI boom faded, in 1989 it merged with American Cimflex, a factory automation firm, to form Cimflex Teknowledge. By the late 1990s Teknowledge was a small company working mostly on research contracts for the US government.`,facts:[`MYCIN, the Stanford expert system of the 1970s, used about 600 rules.`,`Teknowledge merged with American Cimflex in 1989.`]},"company:intellicorpse":{intro:`Modelled on IntelliCorp, a company from Mountain View, California, that began as IntelliGenetics, selling software to help molecular biologists work with DNA sequences. It grew out of Stanford's research on using artificial intelligence in science, and its founders saw a wider market in tools for building expert systems.`,how:`IntelliCorp's main product, KEE, the Knowledge Engineering Environment, ran on Lisp machines and let programmers describe a subject as frames, objects with slots and values, plus rules, with graphics that showed how the system was reasoning. It sold expensive licences to big companies and government agencies building expert systems.`,history:`Stanford researchers, among them Edward Feigenbaum, founded IntelliGenetics in 1980 to sell software for analysing DNA. In 1983 it went public, the first artificial intelligence company to do so, and it released KEE. In the late 1980s the company, by then called IntelliCorp, moved KEE onto cheaper Unix workstations as the market for Lisp machines collapsed. In the 1990s it turned to tools for modelling business processes and for working with SAP's business software.`,facts:[`IntelliCorp's share sale of 1983 was the first by an AI company.`,`IntelliGenetics was founded in 1980 to sell DNA analysis software.`]},"company:interference":{intro:`Modelled on Inference Corporation, a company from Los Angeles founded to sell tools for building expert systems, programs that reason with rules written down from human experts. Its best-known product was ART, the Automated Reasoning Tool, a large software kit for writing such programs.`,how:`ART let programmers write rules of the form if this is true, then do that, and a reasoning engine applied them quickly to the facts of a problem, such as scheduling or checking applications. It ran first on Lisp machines, and Inference sold it, with consulting, to big companies, defence contractors and NASA.`,history:`Inference was founded in Los Angeles in 1979. By 1985 ART was one of the most powerful expert system shells, and NASA's own free tool, CLIPS, written that year, borrowed its style of rules. In the late 1980s Inference rewrote ART in the C language, as ART-IM, to run on cheaper computers. In the 1990s it turned to software for customer service help desks, using a method called case-based reasoning, which matched new problems to records of old ones. In 2000 the customer service software company eGain bought Inference.`,facts:[`NASA's CLIPS of 1985 borrowed its rule style from ART.`,`eGain bought Inference in 2000.`]},"company:sinkingmachines":{intro:`Modelled on Thinking Machines Corporation, a company from Cambridge, Massachusetts, founded by Danny Hillis, an MIT graduate student, and Sheryl Handler to build his Connection Machine. Instead of one fast processor, it used tens of thousands of tiny ones working at the same time, an approach called massively parallel computing.`,how:`The Connection Machine linked up to 65,536 simple processors in a network shaped like a hypercube, so that each could pass messages to others quickly. Programmers gave the same instruction to every processor at once, each working on its own piece of data, which suited jobs such as simulations, image processing and searching text. Most machines were bought with government research money.`,history:`Danny Hillis and Sheryl Handler founded Thinking Machines in 1983, and the physicist Richard Feynman spent summers working there from 1983. In 1986 it shipped the CM-1, with 65,536 processors in a black cube studded with red lights. In 1991 it launched the CM-5, which in 1993 appeared in the film Jurassic Park. Most of its sales depended on DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, and when government spending fell, Thinking Machines filed for bankruptcy in August 1994.`,facts:[`The CM-1 of 1986 had 65,536 tiny one-bit processors.`,`A CM-5 at Los Alamos topped the list of the world's fastest supercomputers in June 1993.`]},"company:kurzwhale":{intro:`Modelled on Kurzweil Computer Products, a company from Cambridge, Massachusetts, founded by the young inventor Ray Kurzweil to build computers that could read printed text in any typeface. Earlier reading machines could handle only one or two special fonts; Kurzweil's omni-font system aimed to recognise ordinary books, magazines and letters.`,how:`The system scanned a page, split the image into letters and recognised each by its shape, whatever the typeface. Its first big use was a reading machine for blind people, which turned the text into synthetic speech and so read a book aloud. The company also sold the recognition system to businesses that needed to turn paper documents into computer text.`,history:`Ray Kurzweil founded the company in 1974. In January 1976 he unveiled the Kurzweil Reading Machine, which combined a flatbed scanner, omni-font character recognition and a speech synthesiser. In 1976 the singer Stevie Wonder bought one of the first machines, and the two became friends. In 1978 the company began selling a version for businesses, and in 1980 Xerox bought it. In 1982 Kurzweil, with Stevie Wonder's encouragement, founded Kurzweil Music Systems to build synthesisers.`,facts:[`The first Kurzweil Reading Machine of 1976 cost about $50,000.`,`Xerox bought Kurzweil Computer Products in 1980.`]},"company:draggin":{intro:`Modelled on Dragon Systems, a speech recognition company founded near Boston by the husband-and-wife researchers James and Janet Baker, who had worked on speech at Carnegie Mellon University and IBM. Their aim was software that turned spoken words into typed text on ordinary personal computers.`,how:`Dragon's software used statistical models, called hidden Markov models, to work out which words were most likely given the sounds it heard, and it learned each user's voice. At first speakers had to pause between every word, a style called discrete speech, and its early customers were doctors, lawyers and people who could not type.`,history:`James and Janet Baker founded Dragon Systems in Newton, Massachusetts, in 1982. In 1990 it released DragonDictate for DOS, which recognised words spoken one at a time. In June 1997 Dragon NaturallySpeaking became the first consumer program to understand continuous, normally paced speech. In 2000 the Bakers sold Dragon to Lernout & Hauspie for shares worth about $580 million, which became almost worthless when L&H collapsed in a fraud scandal that year. In 2001 Dragon's software passed to ScanSoft, which took the name Nuance in 2005. In 2013 a jury rejected the Bakers' lawsuit against Goldman Sachs, which had advised them on the sale.`,facts:[`Dragon NaturallySpeaking cost $695 when it launched in 1997.`,`The Bakers sold Dragon for shares worth about $580 million in 2000.`]},"company:learnout":{intro:`Modelled on Lernout & Hauspie, a Belgian speech technology company founded in Ieper, in Flanders, by Jo Lernout and Pol Hauspie to sell software that recognised speech, translated languages and read text aloud. It promised to make machines understand any language, starting with the many languages of Europe.`,how:`L&H licensed its speech recognition and text-to-speech software to makers of phones, cars, computers and toys, and it grew by buying other language technology companies. It raised money from investors and on the stock market, and it reported fast-rising sales from licensing deals, much of them in Asia.`,history:`Jo Lernout and Pol Hauspie founded the company in Ieper in 1987, with backing from Flemish investors. It listed on the Nasdaq stock market in 1995, and in 1997 Microsoft invested $45 million in it. In 2000 it bought Dragon Systems and Dictaphone, and at its peak that year its shares valued it at about $10 billion. In August 2000 The Wall Street Journal reported that many of its South Korean customers could not be found, and in November 2000 L&H filed for bankruptcy protection. In 2010 a Belgian court convicted its founders of fraud.`,facts:[`Microsoft invested $45 million in Lernout & Hauspie in 1997.`,`L&H was valued at about $10 billion at its peak in 2000.`]},"company:nuisance":{intro:`Modelled on Nuance Communications, which began as a spin-off from SRI International, the research institute in Menlo Park, California, to sell speech recognition for telephone services. It stands too for ScanSoft, the scanning software firm that merged with it, and for the long line of speech and reading technology the two gathered together.`,how:`Nuance sold speech recognition software to banks, airlines and phone companies for automated telephone lines, where callers spoke their requests instead of pressing keys. Its systems used statistical models trained on many recorded voices, and it charged licence fees for each line or system that used them.`,history:`Nuance was spun out of SRI International in 1994. In 2001 ScanSoft, a Massachusetts company built from Xerox's scanning software arm, bought Dragon NaturallySpeaking from the wreck of Lernout & Hauspie. In 2005 ScanSoft bought Nuance and took its name. In 2011 Apple's Siri assistant used Nuance's speech recognition. Through the 2010s it focused on health care, selling dictation software to doctors. In March 2022 Microsoft completed its purchase of Nuance for about $19.7 billion.`,facts:[`ScanSoft bought Nuance and took its name in 2005.`,`Microsoft paid about $19.7 billion for Nuance in 2022.`]},"company:systrain":{intro:`Modelled on Systran, one of the oldest machine translation companies, founded in California by Peter Toma, a Hungarian-born linguist who had worked on early translation programs at Georgetown University. It built computer systems that translated text from one language to another using large dictionaries and grammar rules, at first for the American military.`,how:`Systran's programs analysed the grammar of each sentence, looked up words and phrases in huge hand-built dictionaries and rearranged them by rules into the other language. The results were rough but fast, good enough for experts to get the gist of foreign documents, and government agencies paid Systran to build and maintain each pair of languages.`,history:`Peter Toma founded Systran in 1968, and from 1970 its Russian to English system translated scientific and technical papers for the US Air Force. In 1975 it translated documents for the Apollo-Soyuz mission, the joint American and Soviet space flight. In 1976 the European Commission bought a Systran system for English to French, and it grew to cover many of Europe's languages. In 1997 AltaVista's free Babel Fish service brought Systran's translations to the public on the web. By 2007 Google had replaced Systran's engine in its own translation service with statistical methods. In 2014 the Korean company CSLi bought Systran.`,facts:[`Systran began translating Russian for the US Air Force in 1970.`,`Babel Fish, launched in 1997, offered Systran translations free on the web.`]},"company:hunch":{intro:`Modelled on HNC Software, a company from San Diego founded by Robert Hecht-Nielsen, a pioneer of artificial neural networks, and Todd Gutschow. Neural networks were programs loosely modelled on the brain, which learned patterns from examples instead of following rules written by hand.`,how:`HNC at first sold neural network boards and software to researchers, and then found its market in banks: its Falcon system learned what each cardholder's normal spending looked like and flagged unusual purchases as possible fraud within a fraction of a second. Banks paid for the software and for the scoring of each transaction.`,history:`Robert Hecht-Nielsen and Todd Gutschow founded Hecht-Nielsen Neurocomputer in 1986, building neural network boards for computers. In 1993 it launched Falcon, which used neural networks to spot credit card fraud as transactions happened. It went public in 1995 as HNC Software. By 2000 Falcon was used by many of the largest card issuers in America and abroad. In 2002 Fair Isaac, the credit scoring company, bought HNC for about $810 million in shares.`,facts:[`Falcon, launched in 1993, scored card transactions for fraud as they happened.`,`Fair Isaac paid about $810 million for HNC in 2002.`]},"company:fairish":{intro:`Modelled on Fair Isaac, a company from San Francisco founded by Bill Fair, an engineer, and Earl Isaac, a mathematician, who had met at the Stanford Research Institute. They believed that statistics applied to past records could predict the risk of lending money better than the gut feeling of a bank's loan officer.`,how:`Fair Isaac built scoring systems for lenders: it studied thousands of past loans to find which facts about applicants, such as income, years at one address or past late payments, predicted who would repay, and turned them into a table of points called a scorecard. Lenders paid it to build and maintain these models.`,history:`Bill Fair and Earl Isaac founded the company in 1956. In 1958 it built its first credit scoring system, for a lender called American Investments. In 1989 it introduced the general-purpose FICO score, based on the records held by the big credit bureaus, on a scale from 300 to 850. In 1995 the mortgage giants Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac began recommending FICO scores for home loans, which made them a standard across America. In 2002 it bought HNC Software, and in 2009 it renamed itself FICO.`,facts:[`The FICO score of 1989 runs from 300 to 850.`,`Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac adopted FICO scores for home loans in 1995.`]},"company:chessbass":{intro:`Modelled on ChessBase, a software company from Hamburg, Germany, founded by Matthias Wüllenweber, a physicist, and Frederic Friedel, a science journalist. Its first program was a database for storing and searching chess games, so that players could study openings and their rivals' games on a home computer instead of in books.`,how:`ChessBase sold database software and collections of master games, at first for the Atari ST, along with training discs and playing programs that analysed positions and played against users. Top players used it to prepare for their opponents, and it sold to keen amateurs around the world.`,history:`Matthias Wüllenweber and Frederic Friedel founded ChessBase in 1987, and the world champion Garry Kasparov was among its first users. In 1991 it released Fritz, a chess engine written by Frans Morsch and Mathias Feist. In 1994 Fritz beat Kasparov in a game at a fast-play tournament in Munich. In 2002 Deep Fritz drew an eight-game match with the world champion Vladimir Kramnik in Bahrain. In 2006 Deep Fritz beat Kramnik 4 to 2 in Bonn.`,facts:[`Deep Fritz drew 4 to 4 with Vladimir Kramnik in 2002.`,`Deep Fritz beat Kramnik 4 to 2 in Bonn in 2006.`]},"company:mephisto":{intro:`Modelled on Mephisto, the chess computers made by Hegener + Glaser, an electronics company from Munich founded by Manfred Hegener and Florian Glaser. A Mephisto was a chess board with a hidden microprocessor that worked out its moves, so a player could practise at home against a tireless opponent, and its name came from the devil of the Faust legend.`,how:`Mephisto machines came in many styles, from plastic travel sets to wooden boards with sensors under each square that felt where the pieces stood, and plug-in modules held stronger programs. Hegener + Glaser hired leading chess programmers and sold each machine on its playing strength, proved in tournaments against other computers.`,history:`Manfred Hegener and Florian Glaser founded their company in Munich in 1969 to make semiconductors, displays and portable electronic games. In 1980 it launched the first Mephisto, Germany's first commercial chess computer. Mephisto machines running programs by the British programmer Richard Lang won the World Microcomputer Chess Championship six times between 1984 and 1990. In 1989 Hegener + Glaser bought its American rival, Fidelity Electronics, just as sales of chess computers were falling. In 1994 the British company Saitek bought Hegener + Glaser for about 7 million marks.`,facts:[`Mephisto machines won six world microcomputer chess titles between 1984 and 1990.`,`Saitek bought Hegener + Glaser for about 7 million marks in 1994.`]},"company:netflicks":{intro:`Modelled on Netflix, the DVD-by-post rental company from Los Gatos, California, which set out to win on choice and convenience. Members kept a list of the films they wanted, and a computer system suggested others based on the star ratings they gave to the films they had already watched.`,how:`Netflix asked members to rate films from one to five stars and used those ratings to predict what else they would enjoy, steering them towards older and lesser-known titles that it held plenty of, not just the scarce new releases. Good suggestions kept members subscribing, which mattered more to the business than any single rental.`,history:`Netflix opened its website in 1998, and in 2000 it launched Cinematch, its system for recommending films from members' ratings. In October 2006 it launched the Netflix Prize, offering $1 million to anyone who could make its predictions 10 per cent more accurate, and released 100 million anonymous ratings for contestants. In September 2009 a team called BellKor's Pragmatic Chaos won the prize. Researchers had shown in 2008 that some members could be identified from the supposedly anonymous data, and in 2010 Netflix cancelled a second contest after a privacy lawsuit. In 2011 a plan to split off its DVD service as Qwikster angered customers and was dropped within weeks. In September 2023 Netflix mailed its last DVDs.`,facts:[`The Netflix Prize of 2006 offered $1 million for 10 per cent better predictions.`,`About 100 million film ratings were released for the contest in 2006.`,`Netflix had mailed more than 5 billion DVDs when the service ended in 2023.`]},"company:componentwon":{intro:`Modelled on VideoSoft and APEX Software, the two small American firms behind ComponentOne, which made ready-made controls for programmers. A control was a building block, such as a data grid, chart or calendar, that a developer could drop onto a form in Microsoft's Visual Basic instead of programming it from scratch.`,how:`These firms sold controls as add-ons for Visual Basic, a popular tool for building Windows programs: a developer bought a licence, dropped the control onto a form and set its properties, and could ship it inside finished programs without further fees. Grids that showed and edited database tables were their best sellers, sold by mail order and through developer catalogues.`,history:`Gustavo Eydelsteyn founded VideoSoft in 1991, the year Microsoft launched Visual Basic, and it soon sold one of the first add-on controls for it. APEX Software, founded in 1987, made data-bound grid controls for the same market. In 1994 the Japanese firm GrapeCity began selling their components in Japan. In 2000 VideoSoft and APEX merged as ComponentOne, based in Pittsburgh, and moved on to controls for Microsoft's .NET. In July 2012 GrapeCity bought ComponentOne and made it a division.`,facts:[`VideoSoft was founded in 1991, the year Visual Basic appeared.`,`GrapeCity bought ComponentOne in 2012.`]},"company:infragile":{intro:`Modelled on Infragistics, a maker of user interface controls for programmers from Cranbury, New Jersey, and on ProtoView Development, one of the two companies that formed it. ProtoView, founded by Dean Guida, a former IBM consultant, sold ready-made building blocks such as grids and data tables that programmers could add to their Windows applications.`,how:`ProtoView and its rival Sheridan Software sold controls as add-ons for development tools such as Visual Basic and C++, licensed per programmer, with free use in the programs they built. Their products saved programmers weeks of work on complicated screens, such as spreadsheets, tabbed dialogs and data entry forms.`,history:`Dean Guida founded ProtoView Development in New Jersey in 1989, making tools for C and C++ programmers, while Sheridan Software of Long Island made controls for Visual Basic. In the 1990s Sheridan's tabbed dialog control was included in Microsoft's Visual Basic. In November 2000 ProtoView and Sheridan merged, because both had to rebuild their products for Microsoft's new .NET platform. In 2001 the merged company began trading as Infragistics. In the 2010s it moved into controls for the web and mobile apps.`,facts:[`ProtoView was founded in 1989.`,`The merger that created Infragistics was signed in November 2000.`]},"company:devexcess":{intro:`Modelled on Developer Express, a software company from Glendale, California, started by a group of programmers who had been writing financial programs for banks in Borland's Delphi. They began selling the ready-made controls they had built for their own work, such as data grids, editors and toolbars.`,how:`Developer Express sold its controls as libraries that programmers added to their projects, licensed per developer, with full source code for those who wanted to change them. Its best-known products were data grids that could group, sort and filter records on screen, in the style of Microsoft Outlook.`,history:`Developer Express shipped its first control, a data grid for Delphi, in November 1998. In 2002, as Microsoft launched its .NET platform, it began building controls for it too, starting with the XtraGrid. Through the 2000s it added reporting tools and controls for web pages built with ASP.NET, while it kept selling and updating its Delphi controls. In 2013 it marked 15 years in business, by then known simply as DevExpress.`,facts:[`Developer Express shipped its first Delphi grid in November 1998.`,`DevExpress celebrated 15 years in business in 2013.`]},"company:telltrick":{intro:`Modelled on Telerik, a software company from Sofia, Bulgaria, founded by four young engineers to make user interface controls for Microsoft's new .NET platform. Its components, such as grids, charts and text editors for websites built with ASP.NET, let programmers build rich screens without writing them from scratch.`,how:`Telerik sold its controls in suites licensed per developer, with a year of updates and support. It kept its costs low by developing in Bulgaria while selling mostly to programmers in America and Western Europe, and it added tools for testing software and for managing website content.`,history:`Vassil Terziev, Boyko Iaramov, Svetozar Georgiev and Hristo Kosev founded Telerik in Sofia in 2002. In 2009 it set up the Telerik Academy, which trained young Bulgarian programmers for free. In 2012 it launched Kendo UI, a set of controls written in JavaScript for websites and mobile apps. In 2014 it announced NativeScript, a way to build phone apps with JavaScript. In December 2014 the American company Progress Software completed its purchase of Telerik for $262.5 million.`,facts:[`Telerik had more than $60 million in yearly revenue when Progress bought it in 2014.`,`Progress paid $262.5 million for Telerik in 2014.`]},"company:trolltax":{intro:`Modelled on Trolltech, a software company from Oslo, Norway, founded by Haavard Nord and Eirik Chambe-Eng to sell Qt, a toolkit for building programs with graphical windows, buttons and menus. Programmers could write their code once in C++ and build it for Windows, Unix and other systems, with Qt drawing a native-looking interface on each.`,how:`Trolltech used dual licensing: Qt was free for writing open-source programs, but companies building closed, commercial software had to buy a licence. This brought in both a community of free users, who found bugs and spread the word, and paying customers, who funded the developers.`,history:`Haavard Nord and Eirik Chambe-Eng began designing Qt in 1991 and founded their company, at first called Quasar Technologies, in 1994. Qt 0.90 was released on 20 May 1995. In 1996 the KDE project chose Qt for its free Linux desktop, and arguments over Qt's licence led Trolltech to release it under the GPL, a free software licence, for Linux in 2000. In 2008 Nokia bought Trolltech for about $153 million. In 2012 Nokia sold Qt to the Finnish company Digia, which set up The Qt Company in 2016.`,facts:[`Qt 0.90 was released on 20 May 1995.`,`Nokia paid about $153 million for Trolltech in 2008.`]},"company:beea":{intro:`Modelled on BEA Systems, a software company from San Jose, California, founded by Bill Coleman, Ed Scott and Alfred Chuang, whose first initials gave it its name; Coleman and Chuang came from Sun Microsystems. It sold middleware: software that sits between a company's databases and its applications, handling transactions so that large systems can serve many users reliably.`,how:`BEA started by buying Tuxedo, a transaction processing system created at AT&T's Bell Labs for Unix, and selling it to banks, airlines and phone companies that needed to process huge numbers of transactions. It earned money from licences and yearly maintenance, and its consultants helped customers build systems on its software.`,history:`Bill Coleman, Ed Scott and Alfred Chuang founded BEA in 1995, and in 1996 it bought Tuxedo from Novell. In 1998 it bought WebLogic, a small company whose Java application server ran business programs behind websites. By 2000 WebLogic and IBM's WebSphere were the two leading Java application servers. In October 2007 Oracle offered $6.7 billion for BEA, which said it was worth more, and in January 2008 Oracle agreed to pay about $8.5 billion. The deal closed on 29 April 2008.`,facts:[`Oracle's first offer for BEA, in 2007, was $6.7 billion.`,`Oracle paid about $8.5 billion for BEA, closing the deal in April 2008.`]},"company:irony":{intro:`Modelled on IONA Technologies, a software company spun out of Trinity College Dublin by the computer scientists Chris Horn, Sean Baker and Annrai O'Toole. It made software that let programs running on different computers, written in different languages, call each other across a network as if they were on the same machine.`,how:`IONA's main product, Orbix, was an object request broker built on CORBA, a standard agreed by many computer firms for objects spread across networks. Banks, telephone companies and governments bought licences to tie their old and new systems together, and IONA also sold consulting and support.`,history:`Chris Horn, Sean Baker and Annrai O'Toole founded IONA in Dublin in 1991. In 1993 it released Orbix, one of the first products to follow the CORBA standard. In 1997 it listed on the Nasdaq stock market, one of the biggest Irish technology share sales of its time. At the height of the dot-com boom in 2000 it was valued at up to about $1.75 billion. As web services replaced CORBA its sales shrank, and in 2008 Progress Software bought IONA for about $162 million.`,facts:[`IONA listed its shares on Nasdaq in 1997.`,`Progress bought IONA for about $162 million in 2008.`]},"company:tibcon":{intro:`Modelled on TIBCO, The Information Bus Company, from Palo Alto, California, spun off from Teknekron, the software firm Vivek Ranadivé had founded and sold to the news agency Reuters. Its software, an information bus, carried live data such as share prices to every computer that needed it, the moment it changed.`,how:`TIBCO's messaging used a publish and subscribe model: programs published messages on a subject, such as a stock's price, and any program that had subscribed to that subject received them at once. Banks used it on their trading floors, and other companies used it to link their separate systems, paying TIBCO for licences and support.`,history:`Vivek Ranadivé founded Teknekron Software Systems in 1985, and its information bus spread across the trading floors of Wall Street. In 1994 Reuters bought Teknekron, and in 1997 it set up TIBCO Software as a separate company. TIBCO went public in 1999 and its shares soared in the dot-com boom of 2000. In 2014 the investment firm Vista Equity Partners bought TIBCO for about $4.3 billion. In 2021 it bought Information Builders, and in 2022 it merged with Citrix to form Cloud Software Group.`,facts:[`Reuters bought Teknekron, TIBCO's parent, in 1994.`,`Vista Equity Partners bought TIBCO for about $4.3 billion in 2014.`]},"company:adobo":{intro:`Modelled on Adobe Systems, founded by John Warnock and Charles Geschke, two researchers who left Xerox's Palo Alto Research Center when Xerox showed little interest in their way of describing printed pages. Named after Adobe Creek, which ran behind Warnock's house in Los Altos, it set out to make printers show text and pictures exactly as designed.`,how:`Adobe's PostScript was a programming language for describing pages: a computer sent the printer instructions for drawing letters, lines and pictures, and a PostScript interpreter inside the printer turned them into dots at whatever sharpness the machine could print. Adobe earned royalties on every PostScript printer sold, and sold fonts to go with them.`,history:`John Warnock and Charles Geschke founded Adobe in December 1982, and in 1985 Apple's LaserWriter, the first printer with PostScript built in, helped start desktop publishing. In 1987 it released Illustrator, a drawing program, and in 1990 Photoshop, an image editor written by the brothers Thomas and John Knoll. In 1993 it launched the PDF format and Acrobat, for sharing documents that looked the same everywhere. In 1994 it merged with Aldus, the maker of PageMaker, and in December 2005 it bought Macromedia for about $3.4 billion. In 2013 it stopped selling boxed software and moved fully to Creative Cloud subscriptions, launched in 2012. In 2022 it agreed to buy the design tool Figma for $20 billion, but it dropped the deal in December 2023 after objections from regulators.`,facts:[`Apple's LaserWriter, with Adobe PostScript inside, cost $6,995 in 1985.`,`Adobe paid Figma a $1 billion fee for walking away from their deal in 2023.`]},"company:macromania":{intro:`Modelled on Macromedia, a San Francisco software company formed by merging MacroMind, the maker of the animation program Director, with Authorware, a maker of tools for building training courses. Its programs let designers combine pictures, sound, animation and interaction into multimedia titles for CD-ROMs, kiosks and presentations.`,how:`Macromedia sold authoring tools: Director built interactive titles with a timeline called the score and a scripting language called Lingo, and Authorware built training courses. Designers made the content and shipped it with a free player, while Macromedia earned its money from the tools used to make it.`,history:`MacroMind-Paracomp and Authorware merged in April 1992 to form Macromedia. In 1995 its Shockwave plug-in let Director files play inside web browsers. In 1996 it bought a small animation program called FutureSplash and renamed it Flash. In 1997 it launched Dreamweaver, a visual editor for building websites. By 2005 the Flash Player was installed on most of the world's computers and drove much of the web's animation and video. In December 2005 Adobe completed its purchase of Macromedia for about $3.4 billion.`,facts:[`FutureSplash became Flash when Macromedia bought it in 1996.`,`Adobe paid about $3.4 billion for Macromedia in 2005.`]},"company:quirk":{intro:`Modelled on Quark, a software company from Denver, Colorado, founded by Tim Gill with a loan of $2,000 from his parents. It began by writing programs for the Apple II, among them Word Juggler, one of the first word processors for the machine.`,how:`Quark sold software on disks through computer dealers, starting with word processing and turning to page layout. Its QuarkXPress let designers place text and pictures in boxes on a page with precise control of type, spacing and colour, and printers and publishers paid high prices for it.`,history:`Tim Gill founded Quark in 1981, and in 1986 Fred Ebrahimi joined as co-owner and chief executive. QuarkXPress for the Macintosh was released in March 1987. By the late 1990s it was used for about 90 per cent of professional page layout, from magazines to newspapers. In 1999 Adobe launched InDesign, and in 2000 Tim Gill sold his half of Quark to Ebrahimi and devoted himself to campaigning for gay rights. Quark's high prices, slow move to Apple's Mac OS X and unpopular support policies drove many customers to InDesign during the 2000s. In 2011 Ebrahimi sold Quark to the investment firm Platinum Equity.`,facts:[`Tim Gill started Quark with a $2,000 loan from his parents in 1981.`,`QuarkXPress handled about 90 per cent of professional page layout in the late 1990s.`]},"company:quarrel":{intro:`Modelled on Corel, a Canadian software company from Ottawa founded by Michael Cowpland, a British-born engineer who had already co-founded the telephone equipment maker Mitel. Its name came from Cowpland Research Laboratory, and it began by selling computer systems and desktop publishing set-ups before writing software of its own.`,how:`Corel's drawing software described pictures as lines and curves that could be scaled to any size, and it came on Windows PCs with hundreds of fonts and clip art pictures, at a lower price than rivals on the Macintosh. Corel sold through shops and resellers, and it bought other programs to widen its range.`,history:`Michael Cowpland founded Corel in 1985. In 1989 it launched CorelDRAW, which became the leading drawing program on Windows PCs. In 1996 it bought WordPerfect and the Quattro Pro spreadsheet from Novell, hoping to take on Microsoft Office. In 1999 it launched Corel Linux, a version of the free Debian system, which it gave up in 2001. In 2000 Cowpland resigned, and in 2003 the investment firm Vector Capital bought Corel. In 2019 another investment firm, KKR, bought it, and in 2022 the company renamed itself Alludo.`,facts:[`CorelDRAW was launched for Windows in 1989.`,`Corel bought WordPerfect from Novell in 1996.`]},"company:bohemian":{intro:`Modelled on Bohemian Coding, a small Dutch software company founded by Pieter Omvlee, who began writing programs for Apple's Macintosh while still a student. It made tools for designers, starting with DrawIt, a simple vector drawing program, and sold them online to Mac users.`,how:`Bohemian Coding's programs were built only for the Mac, using Apple's own frameworks, which kept them small and fast. Its best-known program, Sketch, was made for designing screens for apps and websites rather than printed pages, with features such as artboards and reusable symbols, and it was sold online for far less than the big design suites.`,history:`Pieter Omvlee founded Bohemian Coding in 2008 and soon teamed up with the designer Emanuel Sá. Sketch was released on 7 September 2010. In 2012 it won an Apple Design Award. In 2015 the company stopped selling Sketch through the Mac App Store to sell it directly. In 2016 Figma, a rival design tool that ran in a web browser and let teams work on the same file at once, opened to the public and began winning over Sketch's users.`,facts:[`Sketch was released on 7 September 2010.`,`Sketch won an Apple Design Award in 2012.`]},"company:powersnooze":{intro:`Modelled on Powersoft, a software company from Massachusetts that began as Computer Solutions, a one-man contract programming business started by Mitchell Kertzman. It grew by writing business software for small companies' computers, especially programs that planned the materials a factory needed.`,how:`Computer Solutions sold packaged business applications, such as manufacturing planning, to small and medium firms, along with the services to install and support them. Its programmers were always looking for ways to build business programs faster and with less hand-written code.`,history:`Mitchell Kertzman founded Computer Solutions in 1974. In July 1991 it launched PowerBuilder, which let programmers build Windows programs that worked with a database by drawing screens and linking them to data with its DataWindow control. PowerBuilder became one of the most popular client-server tools, and the company went public as Powersoft on 3 February 1993. In February 1995 the database company Sybase completed its purchase of Powersoft for about $904 million in shares. In 2010 SAP bought Sybase, and in 2016 it handed PowerBuilder's development to the software firm Appeon.`,facts:[`PowerBuilder 1.0 was released in July 1991.`,`Sybase paid about $904 million for Powersoft, completing the deal in 1995.`]},"company:misinfo":{intro:`Modelled on Information Builders, a software company from New York founded by Gerald Cohen, Peter Mittelman and Martin Slagowitz. Their product, FOCUS, let business people get reports out of mainframe data by writing short commands in near-English, instead of waiting for programmers to write them in COBOL.`,how:`FOCUS was a fourth-generation language: a user wrote what report was wanted, such as sales by region, and the system worked out how to fetch, sort and total the data. Information Builders sold it for IBM mainframes through licences and yearly fees, and it stayed a privately owned company.`,history:`Gerald Cohen, who had worked on a similar product called RAMIS, left his job in 1975 and founded Information Builders with Peter Mittelman and Martin Slagowitz. By the early 1980s FOCUS was one of the most widely used fourth-generation languages on IBM mainframes, and versions for minicomputers and PCs followed. In the late 1990s it launched WebFOCUS, which delivered reports through web browsers. In January 2021 TIBCO Software completed its purchase of Information Builders.`,facts:[`Information Builders was founded in 1975.`,`TIBCO completed its purchase of Information Builders in January 2021.`]},"company:regress":{intro:`Modelled on Progress Software, which began as Data Language Corporation, founded near Boston by Joseph Alsop, Clyde Kessel and Chip Ziering, three graduates of MIT. They set out to build a language that let programmers write business applications working with a database far faster than in older languages.`,how:`The Progress 4GL combined a programming language with its own database, so one program could define screens, rules and data together, and it ran on many kinds of Unix computers and PCs. Progress sold licences largely through software houses that built packages for particular trades, such as distribution or manufacturing, on top of its tools.`,history:`Joseph Alsop, Clyde Kessel and Chip Ziering founded Data Language Corporation in 1981. In 1984 it released the first commercial version of Progress for Unix, and in 1987 the company took the name of its product. It went public in 1991. In 2008 it bought IONA Technologies, and in 2014 it bought Telerik for $262.5 million. In 2023 a flaw in its MOVEit file transfer software was used by a ransomware gang to steal data from hundreds of organisations. In 2024 it bought ShareFile for $875 million.`,facts:[`Progress paid $262.5 million for Telerik in 2014.`,`Progress bought ShareFile for $875 million in 2024.`]},"company:goofta":{intro:`Modelled on Gupta Technologies, a software company from Menlo Park, California, founded by Umang Gupta, an Indian-born former Oracle executive. It set out to bring relational SQL databases to personal computers and networks of PCs, so that small offices could share data without buying a minicomputer.`,how:`Gupta's SQLBase was a database server that ran on PC networks, and its SQLWindows let programmers build graphical Windows programs that used it, by drawing screens and writing short scripts. It sold licences to companies and software houses moving from terminals to client-server systems.`,history:`Umang Gupta founded Gupta Technologies in 1984, and its SQLBase was one of the first SQL databases for PCs. In 1988 it released SQLWindows, one of the first tools for building client-server programs for Microsoft Windows. In the early 1990s PowerBuilder and Microsoft's Visual Basic took much of its market. In 1996 the company renamed itself Centura Software. In 2001 its database and tools business was sold off and took back the Gupta name.`,facts:[`SQLWindows appeared in 1988, two years before Windows 3.0.`,`Gupta Technologies renamed itself Centura Software in 1996.`]},"company:clarisnt":{intro:`Modelled on Claris, the software company that Apple set up to take over its own applications, such as MacWrite and MacPaint, so that Apple could work with other software makers on equal terms. It sold easy programs for home and small business users of the Macintosh and Apple II.`,how:`Claris sold word processing, drawing, painting and database programs, aimed at people who wanted results without learning to program. It earned money from boxed software sold through computer shops, and it competed with independent software makers for space on the same shelves.`,history:`Apple set up Claris in 1987 and gave it MacWrite, MacPaint and other programs. In 1988 it bought FileMaker, a simple database program, and sold it as FileMaker II. In 1991 it launched ClarisWorks, which combined a word processor, spreadsheet, database and drawing tools in one program. In 1998 Apple took most of Claris back in-house and renamed what remained FileMaker Inc. In 2019 FileMaker Inc took back the Claris name.`,facts:[`Apple set up Claris as a separate company in 1987.`,`ClarisWorks of 1991 put four kinds of office program into one.`]},"company:outsourcery":{intro:`Modelled on OutSystems, a software company founded in Lisbon by Paulo Rosado, a Portuguese engineer, to make building business applications much faster. Its platform let developers draw screens, data and logic in a visual editor and generated the working program for them.`,how:`Developers built applications by dragging and joining blocks in OutSystems' visual editor, and the platform turned them into standard web code, deployed them and kept them updated. Companies paid a yearly subscription based on the size of their applications and number of users.`,history:`Paulo Rosado founded OutSystems in Lisbon in 2001. In 2014 the research firm Forrester coined the term low-code for tools like it, and OutSystems became one of the leading examples. In 2018 the investment firms KKR and Goldman Sachs invested $360 million, valuing it at more than $1 billion. In 2021 a new investment of $150 million valued it at about $9.5 billion.`,facts:[`KKR and Goldman Sachs invested $360 million in OutSystems in 2018.`,`OutSystems was valued at about $9.5 billion in 2021.`]},"company:mendax":{intro:`Modelled on Mendix, a Dutch software company founded in Rotterdam by Derek Roos, Roald Kruit and Derckjan Kruit, who believed that business people and programmers misunderstood each other. Its platform let them build applications together by drawing models of data, screens and processes, which the system turned into working software.`,how:`Mendix sold its platform as a subscription, running the finished applications in its own cloud or on a customer's servers. Its visual models were meant to be clear enough for business experts to follow, while programmers could add their own code where it was needed.`,history:`Derek Roos, Roald Kruit and Derckjan Kruit founded Mendix in Rotterdam in 2005. In 2014 the research firm Forrester named the field low-code, with Mendix among its leaders. In August 2018 the German engineering giant Siemens bought Mendix for about $730 million. From 2018 Siemens used Mendix inside its industrial software, letting factories build apps that read data from their machines.`,facts:[`Siemens paid about $730 million for Mendix in 2018.`,`Mendix was founded in Rotterdam in 2005.`]},"company:bsdizzy":{intro:`Modelled on Berkeley Software Design, or BSDi, a company founded by Rick Adams of UUNET and members of the University of California, Berkeley's Computer Systems Research Group, to sell a version of Berkeley Unix for ordinary PCs with Intel 386 processors. It offered a full Unix, with its source code, for a fraction of the price of commercial versions.`,how:`BSDi sold BSD/386 for $995 including the source code, aimed at internet providers and businesses that wanted a cheap, stable Unix server. Berkeley had rewritten most of the original AT&T Unix code, and BSDi wrote the remaining parts needed to run on PCs and supported customers itself.`,history:`BSDi was founded in 1991, and BSD/386 went on sale in 1992 for $995. In 1992 Unix System Laboratories, the AT&T company that owned Unix, sued BSDi, partly over its advertised phone number, 1-800-ITS-UNIX, and then the University of California too. The case was settled in early 1994 with only a few files removed, but the doubt it cast slowed the spread of BSD while Linux grew. In 2001 BSDi sold its software business to Wind River Systems, which stopped selling BSD/OS in 2003.`,facts:[`BSD/386 cost $995 with full source code in 1992.`,`The Unix System Laboratories lawsuit of 1992 was settled in 1994.`]},"company:suspicious":{intro:`Modelled on SuSE, a company from Nuremberg, Germany, founded by four students, Roland Dyroff, Thomas Fehr, Burchard Steinbild and Hubert Mantel, as a company for software and system development. They offered Unix consulting and sold Linux, the new free Unix-like operating system, on stacks of floppy disks for German users.`,how:`Linux itself was free, so SuSE sold convenience: boxed distributions with the software ready to install, printed manuals and help by telephone. It added its own installation and setup tools to make Linux easier, and it sold support contracts to companies running Linux servers.`,history:`The four founders set up SuSE in Nuremberg on 2 September 1992, and their first Linux product was a German version of Slackware on dozens of floppy disks. In 1994 SuSE released its own distribution, and in 1996 it introduced YaST, its own installation and setup tool. By 2001 it sold SUSE Linux Enterprise Server to businesses. In November 2003 Novell agreed to buy SuSE for $210 million. In 2011 Attachmate bought Novell, and in 2019 the investment firm EQT bought SUSE for about $2.5 billion, making it independent again. In 2021 SUSE listed on the Frankfurt stock exchange, and in 2023 EQT took it private again.`,facts:[`Novell agreed to pay $210 million for SuSE in 2003.`,`EQT bought SUSE for about $2.5 billion in 2019.`]},"company:uncanonical":{intro:`Modelled on Canonical, a company from London founded by Mark Shuttleworth, a South African entrepreneur who had sold his internet security firm Thawte and flown to the International Space Station as a paying tourist. It set out to make Linux easy for ordinary people with a free system called Ubuntu, a southern African word for humanity towards others.`,how:`Ubuntu was based on Debian and released every six months, in April and October, free to download and use. Canonical earned money from support contracts and services for companies, and from versions of Ubuntu for servers, while volunteers around the world helped translate and test it.`,history:`Mark Shuttleworth founded Canonical in 2004, and the first Ubuntu release, 4.10, came out in October 2004. In June 2006 it released its first long-term support version, with updates promised for years. In 2011 it replaced the standard desktop with its own design, Unity, which it gave up in 2017. In 2017 it also dropped its plans for Ubuntu phones. By 2016 Ubuntu was the most widely used system on cloud servers.`,facts:[`Shuttleworth sold Thawte to VeriSign for about $575 million in 1999.`,`The first Ubuntu release came out in October 2004.`]},"company:cygnoose":{intro:`Modelled on Cygnus Support, founded by Michael Tiemann, John Gilmore and David Henkel-Wallace to make money from free software. Its idea was that programs such as the GNU C compiler could be given away and still support a business, by selling help, fixes and custom work to the companies that used them.`,how:`Cygnus adapted the GNU compiler, debugger and other tools to new processors for chip makers and embedded systems firms, which paid for the work, and sold support contracts that promised fixes. It gave its improvements back to the free software projects, so the tools grew better for everyone.`,history:`Michael Tiemann, John Gilmore and David Henkel-Wallace founded Cygnus Support in 1989. In 1995 it created Cygwin, a layer that let Unix tools run on Microsoft Windows. In 1997 its engineers led EGCS, a faster-moving version of the GCC compiler that became the official GCC in 1999. In November 1999 Red Hat agreed to buy Cygnus for shares worth about $674 million.`,facts:[`Cygwin, created in 1995, let Unix tools run on Windows.`,`Red Hat agreed to buy Cygnus for shares worth about $674 million in 1999.`]},"company:bitmoaner":{intro:`Modelled on BitMover, a company founded by Larry McVoy, a programmer who had worked at Sun Microsystems, to build BitKeeper, a version control system for large software projects. Version control keeps every change ever made to a program's files, and BitKeeper let many programmers each keep a full copy of the history and merge their work.`,how:`BitKeeper was distributed: every developer had a whole copy of the project, so they could work offline and swap changes directly, which suited big, scattered teams. BitMover sold licences to companies and offered a free version to open-source projects, on condition that its users did not build rival version control tools.`,history:`Larry McVoy founded BitMover in 1998. In 2002 Linus Torvalds began using BitKeeper's free version to manage the Linux kernel, which upset some free software supporters. In April 2005, after the programmer Andrew Tridgell worked out how to talk to BitKeeper's servers, BitMover withdrew the free version. In April 2005 Torvalds wrote Git, his own version control system, within days to replace it. In 2016 BitMover released BitKeeper as open source.`,facts:[`The Linux kernel was managed with BitKeeper from 2002 to 2005.`,`Linus Torvalds wrote the first version of Git in about ten days in April 2005.`]},"company:unready":{intro:`Modelled on Ready Systems, which began as Hunter & Ready, founded by Colin Hunter and James Ready to sell VRTX, a real-time operating system kernel for microprocessors. A real-time operating system guarantees that a program reacts to events within a fixed time, which matters in machines such as aircraft, factory robots and telephone exchanges.`,how:`VRTX was a small kernel that makers of embedded systems built into their own products, licensed with a fee for each unit shipped. It was sold as a ready-made, tested component, so engineers did not have to write their own task scheduler and could concentrate on their device.`,history:`Colin Hunter and James Ready founded Hunter & Ready in 1980, and VRTX went on sale in September 1981. In the 1980s VRTX ran in many military and space systems, and the young firm Wind River sold networking and development tools that ran on top of it. In 1986 the company renamed itself Ready Systems. In 1993 it merged with Microtec Research, a maker of programming tools. In 1995 Mentor Graphics bought Microtec, and with it VRTX.`,facts:[`VRTX went on sale in September 1981.`,`Mentor Graphics bought Microtec and VRTX in 1995.`]},"company:macroware":{intro:`Modelled on Microware Systems, a software company from Clive, near Des Moines, Iowa, founded by Ken Kaplan to write software for the new microprocessors. It worked closely with Motorola, writing programs such as a version of the BASIC language for its chips.`,how:`Microware's operating system was compact, multitasking and quick to respond, behaving much like Unix but fitting in the small memories of microprocessor systems, with programs stored in modules that could live in read-only memory. It sold licences to computer and device makers, and to hobbyists running it on home computers.`,history:`Ken Kaplan founded Microware in 1977, and its BASIC09 language and OS-9 operating system for Motorola's 6809 chip followed in 1979 and 1980. In 1983 it released OS-9/68K for Motorola's 68000 processors, used widely in industrial and scientific machines. OS-9 also ran on home computers such as the Tandy Color Computer, and in 1991 Philips used it in its CD-i players. In 1999 Microware sued Apple over the name Mac OS 9. In 2001 RadiSys bought Microware.`,facts:[`OS-9 for the Motorola 6809 appeared in 1980.`,`RadiSys bought Microware in 2001.`]},"company:disintegrated":{intro:`Modelled on Integrated Systems Inc, a software company from Sunnyvale, California, founded by the engineer Naren Gupta to sell tools for designing control systems. Its customers were engineers in aerospace, cars and industry who needed to model how a machine would behave before building the electronics that controlled it.`,how:`ISI's MATRIXx let engineers draw a control system as a diagram, simulate it and generate program code to run it. ISI also sold pSOS, a real-time operating system kernel that ran inside embedded devices, licensed to makers of phones, printers, cars and networking equipment.`,history:`Naren Gupta founded Integrated Systems in 1980, and in 1983 it released MATRIXx. In the 1990s its pSOS kernel was one of the most widely used real-time operating systems, found in devices from set-top boxes to telephones. In October 1999 ISI agreed to merge with its rival Wind River Systems, in a deal worth about $417 million. In February 2000 Wind River completed the purchase and soon stopped developing pSOS in favour of its own VxWorks.`,facts:[`MATRIXx, ISI's design tool, dates from 1983.`,`Wind River bought ISI in a deal worth about $417 million, completed in 2000.`]},"company:tormentor":{intro:`Modelled on Mentor Graphics, a company from Oregon founded by Tom Bruggere, Gerry Langeler and Dave Moffenbeier, three managers from the electronics firm Tektronix. It sold computer-aided engineering systems: workstations and software that electronics engineers used to draw, simulate and check designs for chips and circuit boards.`,how:`Mentor at first bundled its design software with Apollo workstations and sold the package to electronics firms, and it sold software on its own as computers grew cheaper. Its tools let engineers test a design in simulation before paying for a chip to be made, and it earned licence and maintenance fees from big chip and electronics companies.`,history:`Tom Bruggere, Gerry Langeler and Dave Moffenbeier founded Mentor Graphics in 1981, and it went public in 1984. In the 1980s Mentor, Daisy and Valid were the leading makers of engineering workstations, known together as DMV. In 1995 it bought Microtec Research, with the VRTX real-time system, and in 2002 Accelerated Technology, the maker of the Nucleus kernel, which took it into embedded software. In 2017 Siemens bought Mentor Graphics for about $4.5 billion, and in 2021 it was renamed Siemens EDA.`,facts:[`Mentor Graphics went public in 1984.`,`Siemens paid about $4.5 billion for Mentor Graphics in 2017.`]},"company:steinbird":{intro:`Modelled on Steinberg, a music software company from Hamburg founded by Karl Steinberg and Manfred Rürup, two musicians, to write programs for recording music on home computers. Using MIDI, a new standard for linking synthesisers and computers, their software turned a computer into a multi-track recorder for the notes played on a keyboard.`,how:`Steinberg's sequencers recorded the notes a musician played on a MIDI keyboard, showed them on screen and let them be edited and played back on synthesisers. It sold its software on disks through music shops, protected against copying by a small hardware key plugged into the computer.`,history:`Karl Steinberg and Manfred Rürup founded Steinberg in Hamburg in 1984, and their first product, Pro-16, ran on the Commodore 64. In 1989 Cubase for the Atari ST showed music as blocks along a timeline, a design many rivals copied. In 1996 Cubase VST added audio effects and instruments as plug-ins, through its Virtual Studio Technology standard, which other companies adopted. In 2003 Pinnacle Systems bought Steinberg, and in 2004 the Japanese instrument maker Yamaha bought it.`,facts:[`Cubase appeared on the Atari ST in 1989.`,`Yamaha bought Steinberg in 2004.`]},"company:digidizzy":{intro:`Modelled on Digidesign, a company from Menlo Park, California, founded by Peter Gotcher and Evan Brooks, two musicians, at first to make sound chips that put real recorded drum sounds into drum machines. It soon turned to editing recorded sound on Apple's Macintosh computer.`,how:`Digidesign combined software with its own audio cards for the Macintosh, which handled the heavy sound processing the computer could not manage alone. Studios bought the hardware and software together, and the company earned money from these systems and their upgrades.`,history:`Peter Gotcher and Evan Brooks founded the company in 1984, at first as Digidrums. In 1985 it released Sound Designer, a program for editing sampled sounds on the Macintosh. In 1989 Sound Tools recorded stereo audio straight to a Mac's hard disk. In 1991 Pro Tools added multi-track recording and mixing, and in 1995 the video editing company Avid bought Digidesign. In 2010 Avid dropped the Digidesign name, by which time Pro Tools was the standard in professional recording studios.`,facts:[`The first Pro Tools of 1991 recorded four tracks.`,`Avid bought Digidesign in 1995.`]},"company:fruityline":{intro:`Modelled on Image-Line, a Belgian software company from Ghent founded by Jean-Marie Cannie, which made games and multimedia software for PCs. It hired a young programmer, Didier Dambrin, who wanted to write a simple drum machine program for Windows.`,how:`Image-Line's music software let users build songs from loops and patterns on a step sequencer grid, with built-in synthesisers and effects, all on an ordinary Windows PC. It sold copies online and promised free updates for life, so a buyer never paid again for new versions.`,history:`Jean-Marie Cannie founded Image-Line in Ghent in 1994. In December 1997 it released FruityLoops, a drum machine program written by Didier Dambrin. In 2003 FruityLoops was renamed FL Studio, partly after a dispute with the maker of Froot Loops breakfast cereal. In the 2010s producers such as Avicii and Martin Garrix made hit records with it. In 2018 FL Studio 20 became the first version to run natively on Apple's Mac.`,facts:[`FruityLoops 1.0 came out in December 1997.`,`FL Studio 20 of 2018 was the first native version for the Mac.`]},"company:abletone":{intro:`Modelled on Ableton, a music software company from Berlin founded by Gerhard Behles, Robert Henke and Bernd Roggendorf; Behles and Henke made electronic music together as Monolake. They wanted software for performing electronic music live on stage, not just for recording it in a studio.`,how:`Ableton's Live arranged loops and recordings in a grid of clips that could be started and stopped in time with the music, and it stretched audio to match any tempo without changing its pitch. It sold licences online and through music shops to producers, DJs and live performers.`,history:`Gerhard Behles, Robert Henke and Bernd Roggendorf founded Ableton in Berlin in 1999, and Live 1.0 came out in 2001. In 2009 it launched Max for Live with Cycling '74, letting users build their own instruments and effects inside Live. In 2013 it launched Push, a pad controller for playing Live, made with Akai. In 2017 it bought Cycling '74, the maker of the Max programming environment. In 2023 Push 3 could run Live on its own, without a computer.`,facts:[`Live 1.0 was released in 2001.`,`Push, launched in 2013, had a grid of 64 pads.`]}};export{e as PEDIA_COMPANIES2};